// The Coach. Curriculum: five strands, six units each.
window.CURRICULUM=[{"strand": "Voice", "intro": "This strand trains the instrument under everything else: breath, consonants, tempo, silence, pitch and size. It works the way you would train a section, mechanism first, then repetition, then the real room. The aim is a voice that stays low, unhurried and audible when the room is loud, the stakes are high or the nerves are up.", "units": [{"id": "voice-1", "title": "Breath and the Phrase", "goal": "Take a low, silent breath at the right boundary and carry a whole thought on it, with the last word as full as the first.", "teach": {"body": "Speech runs on the out-breath. On the in-breath the diaphragm contracts and flattens, the lower ribs swing outward and the belly gives forward. On the out-breath, the voice is not driven by that shape collapsing but by a controlled resistance to it. Singers call this appoggio, from the Italian for leaning: the muscles of inhalation keep a light hold while the abdominals take over, so the air leaves at a steady rate rather than all at once. You already do this. A long note held to the bar line, a bow arm that does not die at the tip, a horn player's air through a phrase: the same management.\n\nSpeech breathing differs from quiet breathing in proportion. At rest, in and out are roughly even. In speech the in-breath is quick and the out-breath long. The inhale should be low (the waist and lower back widen), silent, and taken through a relaxed open mouth at a natural boundary in the thought. Shoulders lifting or an audible gasp signals a high, clavicular breath: shallow, tight in the neck, and the voice thins and rises with it.\n\nThe unit of breath is the sense group, also called the thought group or breath group: a run of words carrying one idea. \"Concert Band 1 rehearses on Tuesday | in M1.3 | from 7.30.\" The breath goes at the boundary, never inside the group. A breath between \"Concert\" and \"Band\" breaks the meaning, and it is exactly how nerves sound from the back of a hall.\n\nTwo failures matter for you. The first is running on residual air: you start a long sentence on half a breath, push to the end, and the last few words, usually the ones that matter, come out pressed, quiet and creaky. The second is the top-up gasp, a breath snatched mid-sentence because the thought outran the plan. A fast associative mind produces long sentences with clauses added in flight, and anxiety stiffens the rib cage and sends the breath high, so both failures arrive together.\n\nThe method. Breathe before the first word, not on it. Let the first word arrive on the start of the exhale with no catch in the throat. Plan each phrase to a comfortable breath, never a maximum one. Refill at every full stop, and at commas where the meaning allows. If a sentence will not fit, shorten the sentence rather than stretching the breath. A breath at a boundary is invisible to an audience. A breath in the wrong place is the only one they hear.", "model": "Good evening, and *welcome*. | Tonight you'll hear all four *concert bands*, | the junior big band | and the string orchestra. || The program runs about *ninety minutes*, | with an interval after the second band. || Please keep phones *silent*, | and enjoy the music.", "mistake": "Good evening and welcome, tonight you'll hear all four concert bands, the junior big band and the, (gasp) string orchestra, and the program runs about ninety minutes with an interval after the second band so please keep your phones on silent and, um, enjoy the music.", "remember": ["Breathe low and silent before the first word.", "Breathe at boundaries, never inside a thought.", "Plan phrases to a comfortable breath, not a maximum.", "Refill at every full stop.", "Shorten the sentence before you stretch the breath."]}, "drills": [{"id": "voice-1-d1", "type": "breath", "title": "Low breath, long release", "how": "Follow each instruction on the pattern shown. The in-breath is silent and low; the out-breath is steady to the very end, with no squeeze at the finish.", "items": ["Hands on the lower ribs at the sides. Breathe in through the mouth until they widen under your hands. Release on a steady \"sss\".", "Same breath. Shoulders stay where they are. Release on \"fff\", keeping the ribs wide as long as you can.", "Same breath. Release on a hummed \"mmm\" at speaking pitch, even in volume from start to finish.", "Same breath. Release on \"zzz\", then let the ribs settle and the next breath fall in by itself.", "Same breath. Release by counting aloud at speaking level, one number per second, and stop before you feel any push."], "pattern": [3, 1, 8, 2], "checks": ["Did your shoulders stay still on every in-breath?", "Was each in-breath silent?", "Did the sound stay even to the end, with no final squeeze?", "Did the next breath arrive on its own rather than being gulped?"]}, {"id": "voice-1-d2", "type": "read", "title": "One thought, one breath", "how": "Read each line aloud at room level. Breathe only at the bars, and land the final word with full voice.", "items": ["Concert Band 1 | rehearses in M1.3 | on Tuesday mornings | from *seven-thirty*.", "The minutes from last meeting | have been circulated, | and I'd like to take them | as *read*.", "Your child has a lesson | every week, | on a rotating timetable, | so it will not always be in the *same* period.", "Our next tune is a ballad | by Billy Strayhorn, | and it's called | *Lush Life*.", "Thank you for coming tonight, | for driving them to rehearsals all year, | and for putting up with the *practice*.", "Item three is the festival budget, | which the treasurer's report covers in full, | and I'll ask for questions | at the *end*."], "checks": ["Did every breath fall on a marked bar and nowhere else?", "Was the final word as strong as the first?", "Were the breaths silent?", "Did any phrase end on squeezed or creaky air?"]}, {"id": "voice-1-d3", "type": "repeat", "title": "Lengthen the phrase", "how": "Say each line three times. First breathe at every bar, then only at every second bar, then on a single breath; keep the last word full on all three.", "items": ["Students will need | their instrument, | their folder | and a pencil | at every rehearsal.", "The council has approved | the new uniform, | the revised fees | and the change | to the meeting schedule.", "We leave the school | at eight, | arrive at the venue | by ten | and play at eleven-thirty.", "Thanks to the band, | to the sound crew, | to the bar staff | and to all of you | for listening.", "The excursion form, | the medical form | and the payment | are all due | on Friday."], "checks": ["On the single-breath version, did the last word still have full tone?", "Did you take the single breath low, before the first word?", "Did you hear where the line wanted to breathe, even when you did not?"]}, {"id": "voice-1-d4", "type": "recall", "title": "Phrase it live", "how": "Read the anchors, then speak the talk in order with the anchors hidden. Breathe at the end of each idea, keep sentences short enough to finish on one breath, and stop cleanly.", "items": ["Welcome to the winter concert / four ensembles tonight / interval after the second / thank the parent committee / phones off", "Why we rehearse before school / the hall is free / students are fresh / it protects their afternoons / attendance is not optional", "Opening the council meeting / welcome and apologies / two items for decision / one for discussion / close by eight", "Between tunes at the venue / name the last tune / who wrote it / what's next / the bar is open", "Parent evening on practice / little and often / a fixed time each day / no nagging, just routine / we will check in each term"], "seconds": 60, "prep": 20, "targets": {"fillers": 3}, "checks": ["Did you breathe at the end of each idea rather than mid-thought?", "Were there any audible gasps or top-up breaths?", "Did the last sentence land with full voice?", "Did any sentence run on with \"and\" to avoid a breath?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Take a concert opening and mark every breath on the page with a bar before you speak. Deliver it at hall level to the back wall, breathing only where you marked. Then deliver it again without the page.", "seconds": 60, "checks": ["Did every breath fall on a mark the first time?", "Without the page, did the breaths still land at boundaries?", "Did the final sentence carry as far as the first?"]}}, {"id": "voice-2", "title": "Consonants and Endings", "goal": "Finish every word that carries meaning, especially the last word of a phrase and every number, name and time, so it survives a reverberant hall or a noisy room.", "teach": {"body": "Vowels carry the sound; consonants carry the meaning. Vowels are long, loud and resonant. Consonants are short and comparatively quiet, and they are where most of the information sits. In a reverberant room this matters more: the tail of each vowel rings on and covers the consonant that follows it. That is why a school hall turns \"fifteen\" into \"fifty\" and \"can\" into \"can't\". The listener at the back hears the vowels perfectly well. What goes missing are the edges.\n\nThe articulators are the lips, the tongue tip, the alveolar ridge (the hard ridge just behind the top teeth), the back of the tongue and the soft palate. The consonants most often lost are the final plosives, /p t k b d g/, where the airflow is stopped and then released. Australian English commonly replaces a final /t/ with a glottal stop (\"tha' 's righ'\"), drops final \"-ing\" to \"-in\", and in connected speech applies elision and assimilation: \"next week\" becomes \"nex week\", \"handbag\" becomes \"hambag\". All of this is natural at a table. In a hall, over 28 instruments being packed away, or down a phone line, it costs words.\n\nThe fix is not to over-enunciate every syllable, which sounds precious and slows you down for no gain. It is to finish the words that matter. Three places earn the effort: the last word of each phrase, because it usually carries the stress and it is where the voice tends to fade; anything a listener has to act on, such as numbers, dates, times, room codes and names; and contrasts, such as \"fifteen, not fifty\", where the difference lives in a consonant and a stress (fif*teen* against *fif*ty).\n\nPhysically: open the jaw on the vowels, and let the tongue tip do the consonant work rather than the jaw. Release a final plosive with a small puff of air into the pause that follows it, the way you would release a note rather than choke it. You already know that a note's cut-off places the time as much as its attack. The consonant ending is the cut-off of a word.\n\nTwo things undo articulation. Speed is the first: when you rush, consonants are the first casualty, because the tongue does not have time to reach its target. Volume is the second: pushing louder tends to widen vowels and blur edges. Slowing slightly and finishing the endings will make you more intelligible at the back of a hall than shouting will.", "model": "The camp bus leaves on *Friday* the *eighth*, | at *eight* o'clock *sharp*. || Forms are due *next* week, | not the week *after*. || If the form isn't *signed*, | the student can't *travel*. || That's *Friday*, | the *eighth*, | at *eight*.", "mistake": "The camp bus leaves on Fri'y the eigh' at eigh' o'clock shar', forms are due nex week no' the week af'er, an' if the form isn' signed they can' travel.", "remember": ["Finish the last consonant of every phrase.", "Land numbers, names, times and rooms cleanly.", "Release final plosives into the pause.", "Open the jaw for vowels; tongue tip for consonants.", "Slow slightly rather than push louder."]}, "drills": [{"id": "voice-2-d1", "type": "repeat", "title": "Release the ending", "how": "Say each item three times: first conversationally, then releasing every final consonant into silence, then at hall volume keeping that release. The vowels should not get longer.", "items": ["That's right.", "Eight o'clock.", "Next week.", "Hand it back.", "Fifteen, not fifty.", "Thirteen, not thirty.", "Bring it on Thursday.", "That's not what I said."], "checks": ["Could you hear the final /t/ or /d/ as a release, not a glottal stop?", "Did the hall version keep the endings as clean as the quiet one?", "Did the vowels stay the same length while the consonants sharpened?"]}, {"id": "voice-2-d2", "type": "read", "title": "Numbers, names, times", "how": "Read each line at room level. Every stressed word must finish cleanly; the listener has to act on it.", "items": ["Rehearsal moves to *M1.3*, | on *Wednesday*, | at *three-fifteen*.", "The fee is *forty* dollars, | not *fourteen*.", "Please welcome | on tenor saxophone, | *Kate Bright*.", "Item *six*, | the *audit* report, | page *eight*.", "The bus leaves at *seven-forty*, | and it will *not* wait.", "That was *Night and Day*, | by Cole *Porter*.", "Payment is due on the *twenty-first*, | the last *Friday* of term."], "checks": ["Would a parent at the back have written down the right number?", "Did every name finish, including its last consonant?", "Did the final word of each line release rather than fade?", "Did \"-teen\" and \"-ty\" sound clearly different?"]}, {"id": "voice-2-d3", "type": "swap", "title": "Glottal to clean", "how": "Say the weak version as written, the way it slips out at speed, then the strong version with the endings finished. Keep the strong version at normal pace.", "items": ["Tha's no' wha' I mean' => That's *not* what I *meant*.", "We star' a' eigh' => We *start* at *eight*.", "Nex week we're mee'in in the hall => *Next* week, | we're *meeting* in the *hall*.", "Pu' i' back in the cupboar' => Put it *back* in the *cupboard*.", "I wan' a decision by Frid'y => I want a *decision* by *Friday*.", "Tha' was the las' tune of the se' => That was the *last* tune of the *set*."], "checks": ["Could you hear exactly which consonants the weak version lost?", "Did the strong version stay at speaking pace, not slow and stagey?", "Did the final word of each strong line release cleanly?"]}, {"id": "voice-2-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Clean over noise", "how": "Imagine 28 students packing up. Give the instruction once, at the level of a room after a cut-off, with every number, name and final word landed.", "items": ["Tell the band where and when the next sectional is.", "Tell parents the three things to bring to camp.", "Tell the council when the next meeting is and what is on it.", "Tell the brass which bars to practise and by when.", "Tell a venue audience the name of the next tune and its composer.", "Tell staff the deadline for concert program entries."], "seconds": 30, "prep": 10, "structure": "ONE", "checks": ["Did every number and name finish cleanly?", "Did you say it once, then stop?", "Did the last word of each sentence carry?"]}], "apply": {"source": "direction", "task": "Take a one-line instruction for the end of a rehearsal and give it to the room once, over imagined packing-up noise. Land every number, place and final consonant, then stop.", "seconds": 15, "checks": ["Did you say it once, without repeating yourself?", "Were the time and the place unmistakable?", "Did you finish the final word rather than let it drop?"]}}, {"id": "voice-3", "title": "Pace and the Clock", "goal": "Set a deliberate tempo for each room, slow down for the one sentence that matters, and fit what you say to the time you have.", "teach": {"body": "Speaking rate is measured in words per minute, and it has two components. Articulation rate is how fast the syllables themselves go. Pause time is how much silence sits between phrases. When people are told to slow down, they usually stretch the syllables, which produces a drawl and sounds either bored or patronising. The better lever is pause time: keep the words at a natural speed and put more silence at the boundaries. The listener hears a calm, unhurried speaker, and the content arrives in clean phrases.\n\nTempo is a choice for the room, and you choose it the way you would count off a tune. As working ranges, not research figures: a council or staff table sits comfortably somewhere around 140 to 165 words per minute. A hall, a concert or an award ceremony wants something slower, around 120 to 145, because of reverberation and distance. Directions in rehearsal can be brisk, as long as each one stops dead. The jazz mic between tunes is the slowest of all: relaxed, laid back, behind the beat. The meter will show you where you are.\n\nTwo things push you faster. Adrenaline speeds the internal clock, so rushing feels like normal pace from the inside. That is ordinary tempo drift, and the cure is the same as in a band: an external reference. The second is a fast mind. When the next thought arrives before this one is finished, speech accelerates to catch it, and a tangent at speed sounds like a lost thread. Finishing the sentence and stopping is the reset.\n\nThe most useful single technique is to slow down for the important sentence. Variation in rate signals importance more than absolute speed does. If everything comes at one tempo, the recommendation, the deadline and the name sound exactly like the preamble. Pause before the key sentence, take it noticeably slower, give its stressed words their full length, and pause after it. Then return to tempo. In music this is rubato: time borrowed for the phrase that needs it.\n\nThe clock is arithmetic. At 140 words per minute, a one-minute concert introduction is about 140 words, including names. A five-minute agenda item is not five minutes of talking; it is your two minutes and their three. Write to the clock and cut before you speak, rather than speeding up at the end to fit what is left.", "model": "Item four is the *instrument hire fee*. || We have two options. | The *first* keeps the fee where it is | and draws on reserves. | The *second* raises it by twenty dollars a term. || || The committee *recommends* | the *second option*. || I'll take questions now.", "mistake": "Item four's the hire fee, we've got two options, one keeps it where it is and uses reserves and the other raises it twenty dollars a term and the committee recommends the second one, so, any questions?", "remember": ["Slow down with pauses, not drawled syllables.", "Count off the tempo for the room before starting.", "Take the key sentence slower, with silence either side.", "Budget about 140 words to the minute.", "Hear yourself rushing: finish the sentence, then stop."]}, "drills": [{"id": "voice-3-d1", "type": "pace", "title": "Table tempo", "how": "Read each item at a council or staff table, keeping the meter inside the range. Get there by pausing at boundaries, not by stretching words.", "items": ["Thanks for coming. We have three items tonight and I'd like to finish by eight. Apologies have been received from two members, and we have a quorum.", "The report has been circulated. In short, enrolments are steady, the instrument fleet needs replacing, and the timetable clash with sport has been resolved.", "I'd like to move to item two. The proposal is to change the concert date to the last Thursday of term, which avoids the school ball.", "Before we vote, let me summarise. The cost is covered, the risk is low, and the students have asked for it. Is there a seconder?", "Staff, a quick one. Program entries for the concert are due on Friday. Use the template and send them to me, not to the office."], "targets": {"wpm": [140, 165], "fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Did the meter stay in range for the whole item?", "Did you slow by pausing rather than by stretching words?", "Did the last sentence of each item keep the same tempo as the first?"]}, {"id": "voice-3-d2", "type": "pace", "title": "Hall tempo", "how": "Read each item as if to a full hall, aiming at the back wall, inside the slower range. Keep the words natural and let the boundaries take the extra time.", "items": ["Good evening, and welcome to the Mount Lawley Senior High School winter concert. We are delighted to have you here.", "Our first ensemble tonight is Concert Band 2. They will be playing three works, and the first is a march.", "This award recognises a student who has given more to the music program than anyone could reasonably ask.", "There will be a twenty-minute interval. Tea and coffee are in the foyer, and the second half begins at the bell.", "Thank you for your support this year. Please travel home safely, and we will see you at the next concert."], "targets": {"wpm": [120, 145], "fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did the meter stay in the hall range?", "Did it sound unhurried rather than slowed down?", "Were the pauses at the ends of sentences longer than at commas?"]}, {"id": "voice-3-d3", "type": "repeat", "title": "Slow the key sentence", "how": "Say each item twice. First at one even tempo throughout; then pause before the final sentence, take it noticeably slower with full-length stressed words, and pause after it.", "items": ["We have looked at the timetable, the staffing and the rooms. || The junior band will move to *Thursday*.", "Your child has been offered a place in the program. || It is a *three-year* commitment.", "The festival had a strong year. Ticket sales were up and costs were down. || We finished with a *surplus*.", "I've listened to every view in the room tonight. || My recommendation is that we *defer* the decision.", "That's the end of the first set. || We'll be back in *fifteen minutes*.", "The ensemble has worked hard all year. || Tonight is the first time they have played this piece *in public*."], "checks": ["On the second version, was the key sentence clearly slower than the rest?", "Did you pause both before and after it?", "Did you return to tempo, or let the slowness leak into what followed?"]}, {"id": "voice-3-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Talk to the clock", "how": "Speak for exactly the time given, at table tempo, and slow down for the now-what sentence. Finish on time, not early by rushing or late by trailing.", "items": ["The state of the instrument fleet.", "Why rehearsal attendance has dropped this term.", "What the festival association needs from its members this year.", "The result of the Year 7 auditions.", "A change to the concert schedule.", "What the music department needs from the principal next year."], "seconds": 60, "prep": 15, "structure": "WSN", "targets": {"wpm": [135, 160], "fillers": 3, "pauses": 4}, "checks": ["Did you finish within five seconds of the time?", "Was the now-what sentence noticeably slower than the rest?", "Did the meter stay in range in the final fifteen seconds?", "Did you stop after the last sentence rather than add another?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Take a parent evening passage. Before speaking, find the one sentence parents must remember. Deliver the passage at hall tempo in the time given, slowing and pausing for that sentence only.", "seconds": 60, "checks": ["Did the meter stay in the hall range?", "Was the key sentence clearly slower, with silence either side?", "Did you finish inside the time without speeding up at the end?"]}}, {"id": "voice-4", "title": "The Held Beat", "goal": "Stop at the full stop and stay stopped, place a pause before the word that matters, and wait out a room without filling the silence.", "teach": {"body": "A pause is a rest, and a rest has a value. You would never let a band play through a notated rest, or rush the held beat after a cadence. Speech needs the same discipline, and the rhetorical pause has four distinct jobs.\n\nThe first is the full-stop pause: the grammatical boundary at the end of a sentence. It tells the listener one idea has finished and gives them time to file it. Hold it for about a beat. The second is the pause before the key word, which creates expectation: \"The award goes to | Mia.\" The half-second of silence makes the next word the most important thing in the room. The third is the pause after a key line, which lets it land; two beats, sometimes more, after a decision, a number or a name. The fourth is waiting out a room: standing still and saying nothing until it is quiet, rather than talking into noise or asking for silence. You already do this with a baton raised. The same stillness works at a lectern.\n\nThe enemy of the pause is the filled pause: \"um\", \"uh\", \"so\", \"and\", \"you know\". Speakers fill silence while planning the next phrase, partly to signal that they have not finished and still hold the floor. In a room you chair, you do not need that signal. Silence holds the floor perfectly well if three things stay constant: your body is still, your eyes stay on the room, and you take a breath in the gap. A pause looks like a lost memory only when the eyes drop, the body shifts, or a filler leaks out at the end of it.\n\nSilence feels much longer from the inside than it sounds from the outside, especially when anxious. Two seconds after a question feels like an age to you and like nothing to a council. When you ask \"Any questions?\", count five in your head before saying anything, and do not answer your own question. The answer usually arrives at about four.\n\nThe common errors. Pausing before trivial words rather than key ones. The trailing \"and\" that joins sentences so you never have to stop. Moving during the pause, which reads as uncertainty. And waiting out a room with an expression that asks for permission; neutral and patient is the face, the same one you would use waiting for a section to settle.", "model": "Our final award tonight | is the one the staff *argue* about most. || This year's award for *outstanding contribution* || goes to a student who has played in *every* ensemble we run, | and never once missed a rehearsal. || || Please welcome | *Mia Chen*.", "mistake": "This year's award for outstanding contribution goes to, um, a student who's, you know, played in pretty much every ensemble and, so, never missed a rehearsal, so please welcome Mia Chen.", "remember": ["Stop at the full stop, and stay stopped.", "Pause before the word you want heard.", "Hold still and keep your eyes up in silence.", "Wait for quiet; never talk into noise.", "Replace every \"um\" with a breath."]}, "drills": [{"id": "voice-4-d1", "type": "hold", "title": "Hold after the full stop", "how": "Say each line, then hold silence for the count shown with your body still and your eyes on an imagined room. Take a quiet breath during the hold.", "items": ["The meeting is open.", "That concludes the treasurer's report.", "We will not be changing the date.", "That was the last tune of the first set.", "Thank you, everyone. That's the end of rehearsal.", "The motion is carried."], "hold": 2, "checks": ["Did you hold the full count without moving?", "Did any sound leak out at the end of the hold?", "Did your eyes stay up rather than drop?"]}, {"id": "voice-4-d2", "type": "read", "title": "The pause before the word", "how": "Read each line, placing a clear pause at the bar just before the stressed word. The pause should sound deliberate, not lost.", "items": ["The winner of this year's composition prize | is *Leo Park*.", "What this program needs most | is *time*.", "The committee has decided | to *proceed*.", "The thing your child will remember | is the *concert*, | not the practice.", "Please welcome to the stage | the *Junior Big Band*.", "The answer, | in a word, | is *no*."], "checks": ["Was there a clear silence before each stressed word?", "Did the stressed word arrive firmly after the pause?", "Did the pause sound chosen rather than searched for?"]}, {"id": "voice-4-d3", "type": "hold", "title": "Wait out the room", "how": "Say each line as an invitation to the room, then hold silence for the full count. Do not answer your own question, rephrase it or fill the gap.", "items": ["Are there any questions on the budget?", "Who hasn't yet handed in their camp form?", "Before we vote, does anyone wish to speak against?", "Trumpets, what's marked in bar forty?", "Is there anything else anyone needs from me tonight?", "Does anyone have a reason we shouldn't go ahead?"], "hold": 5, "checks": ["Did you hold the full five without speaking?", "Did you stay still with a neutral face?", "Did you resist answering or rephrasing your own question?", "Did your question end down, as an invitation rather than a plea?"]}, {"id": "voice-4-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Silence instead of fillers", "how": "Speak on the topic using the structure shown. Every time you feel a filler coming, close your mouth and pause instead; let the meter count pauses, not ums.", "items": ["Why the music program matters to the school.", "What makes a good rehearsal.", "The best gig you have ever played.", "Why the festival should keep its current venue.", "What you look for in a Year 7 audition.", "How you run a meeting that finishes on time."], "seconds": 45, "prep": 15, "structure": "PREP", "targets": {"fillers": 0, "pauses": 6}, "checks": ["Did the filler count stay at zero, or close to it?", "Did you pause at least six times?", "Did each pause stay still and silent, with eyes up?", "Did you finish on the point, then stop?"]}], "apply": {"source": "scenario", "task": "The hall is full of chatter and you need to start the concert. Say aloud how you will get silence without asking for it, then do it: walk to the mic, stand, wait, and speak your first sentence only when the room is quiet.", "seconds": 45, "checks": ["Did you avoid \"shh\", \"okay\" or asking for quiet?", "Did you stay still and neutral while waiting?", "Did your first sentence come only after the room settled?"]}}, {"id": "voice-5", "title": "Pitch and the Downward Close", "goal": "End statements down, turn disguised questions into plain statements, and keep rising pitch for genuine questions only.", "teach": {"body": "Every sense group has a nucleus, also called the tonic syllable: the stressed syllable where the main pitch movement happens, usually on the last important word. The direction of that movement carries meaning. A fall signals completion and certainty: this is finished, this is so. A rise signals that something is incomplete: a question, a list with more to come, or a check that the listener is following. Over a whole sentence the voice also drifts gradually lower, a pattern called declination, which is part of why a well-ended sentence sounds settled.\n\nThe rise used on statements is called the high rising terminal, or uptalk. It is well documented in Australian English, and in conversation it does a real job: it checks the listener is with you. In a council chair, the principal's office or a parent meeting, a rising statement reads as asking permission. \"We'll start at seven?\" invites negotiation over something you have decided.\n\nThe related trap is the question that is not a question. \"Can we pack up?\" to 28 students is an instruction disguised as a request, and it gets the response a request gets. \"Pack up now\" with a fall gets the response an instruction gets. The reverse applies too: a genuine question should sound like one. Yes or no questions usually rise; questions beginning with what, when, who or why usually fall, and \"Any questions?\" can fall too, which makes it an invitation from someone in charge rather than a hope.\n\nLists are the one place statements legitimately rise: each item rises slightly, and the final item falls to signal the end. \"Bring your instrument, your folder, and a pencil\", with the fall on \"pencil\".\n\nAnxiety raises pitch overall, because tension lifts the larynx, and hedging pulls statement endings upward. The fix is physical and musical. Think of the end of a statement as a cadence that resolves to the tonic: the last stressed word steps down to a comfortable low note in your range and stays there. Do not press it down to the floor; forcing the pitch below your comfortable range produces vocal fry, a creaky rattle that sounds tired rather than certain. The fall should be a clean step on the nucleus, then silence.\n\nThe meter tracks end-of-phrase pitch rise. On statements, it should read flat or falling. If it shows a rise, find which word carried it and say that sentence again.", "model": "I'm asking for a *second* rehearsal room on Tuesdays. || The junior band has grown to *forty*. | The current room holds *thirty*. || I've checked the timetable, | and M2.1 is *free*. || I'd like a decision by the *end of term*.", "mistake": "So I was wondering about maybe a second room on Tuesdays? Because the junior band's at forty now? And the room holds thirty? So if we could get a decision by the end of term?", "remember": ["End statements down, on the last stressed word.", "Step down to a comfortable low note, not the floor.", "Give instructions as instructions, not questions.", "Rise on list items; fall on the last.", "Ask real questions; state everything else."]}, "drills": [{"id": "voice-5-d1", "type": "repeat", "title": "Rise, then fall", "how": "Say each line three times: first with a rising end, then with a clean fall on the last stressed word, then with the fall followed by a held beat. Listen to how the meaning changes.", "items": ["Rehearsal starts at seven.", "The fee is staying the same.", "I'll need that by Friday.", "We're not changing the program.", "That's the end of the set.", "I recommend we defer it."], "checks": ["Could you hear the rising version asking permission?", "On the fall, did the pitch step down on the last stressed word?", "Did the fall stay clear of creak or fry?", "Did the meter show no end rise on the second and third versions?"]}, {"id": "voice-5-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Question into statement", "how": "Say the weak version with its natural rise, then the strong version with a clean fall. Keep the strong one warm; firm is not cold.", "items": ["We'll start at seven? => We'll start at *seven*.", "Can we pack up? => Pack up *now*, please.", "I was thinking we might defer it? => I recommend we *defer* it.", "Is it okay if I take questions at the end? => I'll take questions at the *end*.", "Could we maybe get a second room? => I'm asking for a *second room*.", "So that's sort of the plan? => That's the *plan*."], "checks": ["Did every strong version end with a fall?", "Did you drop the hedges as well as the rise?", "Did the strong versions still sound courteous?"]}, {"id": "voice-5-d3", "type": "read", "title": "Lists that land", "how": "Read each list with a slight rise on every item except the last, then a clean fall on the final item and a held beat.", "items": ["Bring your *instrument*, | your *folder*, | and a *pencil*. ||", "Tonight we'll hear the *strings*, | the *concert bands*, | and the *big band*. ||", "The report covers *income*, | *expenses*, | and the *reserve*. ||", "We need *volunteers*, | *sponsors*, | and a new *venue*. ||", "On bass, *Sam Ng*; | on drums, *Ali Shah*; | and on piano, *Ruth Lee*. ||", "Arrive at *five*, | warm up at *six*, | play at *seven*. ||"], "checks": ["Did each item before the last rise slightly?", "Did the final item fall clearly?", "Did you hold the beat after the list?"]}, {"id": "voice-5-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Stating a position", "how": "Speak to the decision-maker named in the topic using the structure shown. Every statement ends down; the only rise allowed is a genuine question.", "items": ["To the principal: why the department needs a Level 3 role.", "To the council: why the fee should rise.", "To a parent: why their child cannot withdraw mid-year.", "To the festival committee: why the budget needs a contingency line.", "To staff: why concert week needs a timetable change.", "To a journalist: what the music program does for the school."], "seconds": 60, "prep": 20, "structure": "PREP", "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did the meter show no end-of-phrase rise on statements?", "Did your final point fall and then stop?", "Did any hedge (\"sort of\", \"maybe\", \"I think\") pull an ending up?", "Did any genuine question sound like a question?"]}], "apply": {"source": "pair", "task": "Read the weak phrasing aloud with its natural rise, then the strong phrasing with every statement falling. Then say the strong version from memory, to an imagined principal, with a held beat at the end.", "seconds": 30, "checks": ["Did every statement in the strong version end down?", "Did the memory version keep the fall?", "Did you hold the beat at the end instead of adding a softener?"]}}, {"id": "voice-6", "title": "Projection Without Shouting", "goal": "Size your voice to any room, from a council table to a hall, a rehearsal room or a microphone, with power from the breath and a free throat.", "teach": {"body": "Loudness in the voice comes from breath pressure beneath the vocal folds and from how efficiently the folds close. Carrying power comes from resonance: the throat and mouth shaping and amplifying that sound. Shouting gets loudness from the wrong place. The larynx rises, the neck tightens, pitch climbs and the folds collide harder. It sounds strained and it tires the voice quickly. Teachers are among the occupational groups at highest risk of voice disorders, and this is the usual mechanism.\n\nProjection is more support, not more throat. The breath from unit one does the work: a low breath and a firm, steady out-breath raise the pressure. The throat stays open and the jaw loose. Consonants from unit two do the rest, because intelligibility at distance depends on them more than on volume. Think of directing the voice to a target, the back wall or the farthest person, rather than pushing it out. Human hearing is most sensitive in the upper-middle frequencies, roughly where the bright ring in a trained voice sits, so a forward, slightly bright tone carries further than a dark, pushed one at the same effort. Opera singers carry over an orchestra this way, not by shouting.\n\nIn noise, people automatically speak louder and higher. This is the Lombard effect, and over a rehearsal it is exactly how a voice gets worn out. The answer is not to compete. Cut the band off with the baton, wait for silence, then speak at a level just above a quiet room, not above the band.\n\nEach room has its size. A hall: aim at the back wall, slow down, shorten the phrases, finish the consonants. A rehearsal room: cut off first, then speak. A council table: conversational level, aimed at the farthest member. Projecting at a table reads as lecturing or aggression, which is the opposite of chairing. A microphone: let it do the work. Speak at conversational level, keep a constant distance, and do not lean in or turn your head away, because level changes sharply with distance. Speak slightly across the mic rather than straight into it, to avoid plosive pops on /p/ and /b/. Directional mics also boost bass the closer you get, the proximity effect, which matters with a low speaking voice: too close and you become boomy and muddy.\n\nThe test of good projection is that it costs nothing to the throat. If your neck tightens, the volume is coming from the wrong place.", "model": "*Thank* you. || | Trumpets, | bar *sixty*. | That's marked *forte*, | not fortissimo. | Let the saxes *through*. || Everyone else, | well *played*. || Breathe at the *rest*, | watch for the cut-off, | and give me the *crescendo* at sixty-four. || From bar *fifty-two*.", "mistake": "(Shouted over the band, still playing) TRUMPETS! TRUMPETS, BAR SIXTY, YOU'RE TOO LOUD! EVERYONE, HOLD ON, HOLD ON!", "remember": ["Power from the breath, never the throat.", "Aim at the farthest listener, not the front row.", "Stop the band, then speak.", "Conversational level at a table and on a mic.", "Keep mic distance constant and speak across it."]}, "drills": [{"id": "voice-6-d1", "type": "breath", "title": "Supported count", "how": "Follow each instruction on the pattern shown. The volume should rise from the waist and ribs working; the throat and neck stay exactly as they are at rest.", "items": ["Breathe low. Count one to six on the out-breath at council-table level.", "Breathe low. Count one to six aimed at the far wall of a classroom.", "Breathe low. Count one to six aimed at the back row of a hall, feeling the waist firm rather than the neck.", "Breathe low. Count one to six starting at table level and growing to hall level by six, with no rise in pitch.", "Breathe low. Count one to six starting at hall level and shrinking to table level, keeping the consonants clean."], "pattern": [3, 0, 6, 2], "checks": ["Did the neck stay relaxed at hall level?", "Did pitch stay level as volume grew?", "Did you feel the effort at the waist and ribs, not the throat?"]}, {"id": "voice-6-d2", "type": "repeat", "title": "Size to the room", "how": "Say each line four times, resizing for each room in turn: a council table, a classroom, a full hall, then a rehearsal room just after a cut-off. Only the size changes; pitch and tempo stay put.", "items": ["Good evening, everyone.", "Let's begin.", "From bar thirty-two, please.", "Thank you all for coming.", "That's the end of tonight's program.", "I'd like to hear from those who haven't spoken yet."], "checks": ["Were the four sizes clearly different?", "Did pitch stay put as the size grew?", "Did the table version stay conversational rather than projected?", "Did the hall version stay free of strain?"]}, {"id": "voice-6-d3", "type": "mirror", "title": "Throat stays free", "how": "Use the front camera as a mirror. Follow each instruction, watching your neck, jaw and shoulders for the whole time shown.", "items": ["Say \"Good evening, everyone\" at table level, then at hall level. Watch the neck: no cords standing out at hall level.", "Hand on the front of your throat. Count to ten growing louder. The larynx should not climb under your hand.", "Watch your jaw while saying \"From bar fifty-two\" at hall level. It drops for the vowels and stays loose.", "Watch your shoulders while taking a breath for the hall. They do not rise.", "Speak a concert welcome at hall level. Keep your chin level, not jutting forward to reach the back."], "seconds": 20, "checks": ["Did the neck stay smooth at hall level?", "Did the chin stay level rather than push forward?", "Did the shoulders stay down on every breath?"]}, {"id": "voice-6-d4", "type": "read", "title": "The mic between tunes", "how": "Hold the phone like a mic, a constant hand-span away and slightly off to one side. Read at conversational level, laid back, letting the mic carry it.", "items": ["Thanks very *much*. || That was *Body and Soul*, | and I'm *Dijon Summers* on bass.", "On piano tonight, | *Ruth Lee*. || On drums, | *Ali Shah*.", "This next one is a *ballad*, | so you'll hear the *bar* more than the band. || Be *kind*.", "We're going to take a short *break*. || Back in *fifteen*.", "The festival runs all *weekend*, | and the *program* is on the table by the door.", "Last one for tonight. || Thank you for *listening*."], "checks": ["Did the distance from the phone stay constant throughout?", "Did the /p/ and /b/ sounds stay free of pops?", "Did you stay at conversational level rather than projecting?", "Did names and titles still finish cleanly?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Deliver a concert closing to the back of the largest room you can get into, without a microphone, with power from the breath. Then deliver it again holding your phone as a mic at a constant distance, at conversational level.", "seconds": 60, "checks": ["In the first delivery, did the throat stay free at full size?", "In the second, did you stop projecting and let the mic work?", "Did the consonants carry in both?", "Did your voice feel fresh at the end of both?"]}}]}, {"strand": "Train of thought", "intro": "This strand trains the line through what you say: the point first, a shape you can hold, and the signposts that let you leave the line and find it again. A fast associative mind is an asset in a room only when the room can follow it, and when you can too.", "units": [{"id": "thought-1", "title": "One point, said first", "goal": "Open any contribution with a single headline sentence that carries the whole point, so everything after it is optional support.", "teach": {"body": "The military calls it BLUF: bottom line up front. Journalists call it the lede. Barbara Minto built the Pyramid Principle on it: the answer goes at the top and the reasoning hangs underneath. The mechanism is the same in every version. You say the conclusion in one sentence before you say anything else, and every sentence after that is support the room can take or leave.\n\nIt matters more for a fast associative mind than for anyone. Your thinking runs as a web: one idea lights up five others, and they all feel relevant because they are. If you start speaking at the beginning of that web, with the background, you are walking the room through your search for the point. Somewhere around the third connection the point gets lost, or arrives so late that the principal has already decided what you meant. If you start with the point, the web becomes an asset: any tangent you take afterwards hangs off a line the room already holds.\n\nThe headline sentence has three properties. It is a claim, not a topic: \"The concert needs to move to the 14th\" rather than \"I wanted to talk about the concert date\". It fits in one breath. And it is the sentence you would keep if you were only allowed one. Test it by asking: if I were cut off after this sentence, would they know what I want?\n\nWhat goes wrong. You open with context because it feels polite or safe. You open with a hedge (\"This might be a bit of a tangent, but\"). You open with the story of how you got here. All three spend the room's attention before you have given it anything to attach to. In a council meeting, the chair who says \"I'd like us to approve the budget tonight\" and then explains is followed; the chair who explains and then asks is interrupted.\n\nHow to do it. Before you speak, find the sentence. Not the whole contribution: the sentence. Say it, land it with a falling pitch, and pause. That pause is where the room registers the point and where you choose what support it needs. Often it needs less than you think. Sometimes the headline is the whole contribution, and the strongest move is to stop.", "model": "I'd like us to *move* the winter concert to the *fourteenth*. || The hall is double-booked on the seventh, | and the fourteenth gives the bands a *full* extra week. || That's the proposal.", "mistake": "So, I was looking at the hall bookings last week, and there's a bit of an issue, because the drama people have it on the seventh, which I didn't know, and so I was thinking maybe we could look at other dates, possibly the fourteenth?", "remember": ["Say the conclusion before the context.", "Make it a claim, not a topic.", "One breath, falling pitch, then pause.", "If cut off now, would they know?", "Sometimes the headline is the whole answer."]}, "drills": [{"id": "thought-1-d1", "type": "read", "title": "Headlines, then a beat", "how": "Read each headline aloud, landing the stressed word, and hold the full stop before any support. Notice how complete the headline sounds on its own.", "items": ["We need a *second* bus for Nannup. || The first one is full.", "I'm recommending we *keep* the fees where they are. || Here's why.", "Year 9 ensembles will rehearse on *Thursdays* next term. || Monday doesn't work for the hall.", "The festival made a *surplus* this year. || A small one, | but a surplus.", "Your son is *ready* for Concert Band 2. || I'd like to move him up.", "Brass, | I need *shorter* notes in bar forty. || Again from thirty-six."], "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did the headline end lower than it began?", "Did I pause before the support sentence?", "Could the headline stand alone without the second sentence?"]}, {"id": "thought-1-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Context first, point first", "how": "Say the weak version as written, then the strong version. Listen for where the point arrives in each.", "items": ["So I've been looking at the numbers and talking to a few people and I think maybe we should consider a second rehearsal => We need a second rehearsal before the concert. | The numbers are close.", "Just a bit of background on where we are with the uniform order, there's been some back and forth => The uniforms will arrive in week six. | Two weeks later than planned.", "I suppose what I'm getting at, in a roundabout way, is the timing => The timing is the problem.", "There are a lot of factors here and it's complicated, but on balance => I'm recommending we say no.", "I don't know if this is the right forum, but I wanted to raise something about the budget => I'd like ten minutes on the budget before we close.", "We've had some feedback from parents and some of it is about the start time => Parents want the concert to start at six, not seven."], "checks": ["Did the strong version put the claim in the first five words?", "Was the strong version said in one breath?", "Did I resist adding a hedge to the strong version?"]}, {"id": "thought-1-d3", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Find the sentence, say it", "how": "Use the prep time only to find the headline sentence. Say it, pause, then stop. Nothing else.", "items": ["The principal asks: how did the jazz cellar night go?", "A council member asks where the school stands on the new instrument hire fee.", "A parent asks whether their daughter should keep up piano as well as clarinet.", "The festival committee asks for your view on moving to a two-day format.", "A colleague asks what you thought of the new timetable draft.", "A Year 8 asks why they have to play scales."], "structure": "ONE", "prep": 5, "seconds": 10, "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Was my first sentence a claim rather than a topic?", "Did I stop after the headline instead of explaining?", "Did I avoid opening with \"so\", \"well\" or \"I think\"?"]}, {"id": "thought-1-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Headline, then support", "how": "Open with the headline, pause, give at most two sentences of support, then restate the headline in different words and stop.", "items": ["The IMSS council asks whether instrumental lessons should move out of class time.", "The executive asks why Music needs a bigger share of the performing arts budget.", "A journalist asks why school music matters when results are under pressure.", "A parent at the information evening asks whether the specialist program is worth three years.", "The AJEA board asks whether the festival should raise ticket prices.", "Staff meeting: you are asked for one improvement to reporting."], "structure": "PREP", "prep": 10, "seconds": 40, "targets": {"fillers": 2, "wpm": [130, 165]}, "checks": ["Did the headline arrive before any background?", "Did I pause after the headline?", "Did the close restate the point rather than add a new one?", "Would the room still know my point if I'd been cut off at ten seconds?"]}], "apply": {"source": "scenario", "task": "Read the scenario, decide your position, and answer aloud with the headline as your first sentence. Then answer again, cutting everything after the headline that the room does not strictly need.", "seconds": 45, "checks": ["Was my first sentence the decision, not the background?", "Was the second attempt shorter than the first?", "Did I end on the point rather than trail off?"]}}, {"id": "thought-2", "title": "Structures you can hold", "goal": "Choose PREP, TWO, WSN or PCF in the first second of a question and use it as rails, so the third point never goes missing.", "teach": {"body": "Working memory is small. The well-established finding is that people can hold only a handful of items in mind at once, and under stress that handful shrinks. Adrenaline, a room watching you, and a mind that keeps generating new connections all compete for the same space. A structure is a way of storing your talk outside working memory: you hold one shape, not five ideas, and the shape tells you what comes next.\n\nFour shapes cover almost everything you will be asked.\n\nPREP: point, reason, example, point. Use it when you have a position and need to persuade. \"We should keep the Thursday slot. The hall is free and the kids are fresh after lunch. Last term Thursday rehearsals were our best attended. So, Thursday.\" The repeated point at the end is not redundancy; it is the landing.\n\nTWO: two things, first, second, so. Use it when you have more than one thing to say and fear losing count. Two is the number you can always hold. Three is safer on paper and riskier in the moment, because the third is the one that evaporates. If you genuinely have three, say \"two things\" and fold the third into the \"so\".\n\nWSN: what, so what, now what. Use it to report: the festival result, the audition numbers, the incident at rehearsal. What happened, why it matters, what we do next. It stops a report becoming a narrative, and it ends on an action, which is what committees want.\n\nPCF: problem, cause, fix. Use it when something is broken and you are proposing the repair. Name the problem in one sentence, the cause in one, then spend your time on the fix. It keeps you out of the history of the problem, which is where tangents breed.\n\nHow to choose. In the first second, ask one question: am I arguing, listing, reporting or fixing? Arguing is PREP. Listing is TWO. Reporting is WSN. Fixing is PCF. Do not deliberate; pick one and commit, because a slightly wrong structure held firmly beats the right content held loosely.\n\nWhat goes wrong. You choose a structure and abandon it halfway because a better idea arrives. Let it arrive, and finish the shape anyway. The other failure is announcing the structure and then not following it: if you say \"two things\", the room will count.\n\nPractise these until the choice is reflexive, the way a key centre is reflexive when you read a chart.", "model": "*Two* things about the new timetable. || *First*, | Concert Band 1 loses its Friday slot, | which is our only full-band rehearsal. || *Second*, | the change lands in week three, | right before the festival. || *So* | I'm asking for one more term on the old timetable.", "mistake": "There's a few things with the timetable, I mean the Friday thing obviously, and also the timing, and there was a third thing, what was it, anyway, the main thing is it's not great.", "remember": ["Arguing: PREP. Listing: TWO. Reporting: WSN. Fixing: PCF.", "Choose in one second, then commit.", "Say two, not three.", "Finish the shape even when a better idea arrives.", "Announce it only if you will follow it."]}, "drills": [{"id": "thought-2-d1", "type": "read", "title": "Hear each shape", "how": "Read each model aloud. Stress the structural words so you hear the skeleton under the content.", "items": ["PREP. | We should *keep* the jazz night. || It's our best fundraiser. || Last year it covered the whole Nannup bus. || So we *keep* it.", "TWO. | *Two* things before we start. || *First*, | phones in bags. || *Second*, | we're starting at letter C. || *So*, | phones away, | letter C.", "WSN. | The auditions *filled* every place. || *That* means we start the year with a waiting list. || *Now* we need to decide who gets the first offer.", "PCF. | The *problem* is late arrivals on Tuesday. || The *cause* is the bus timetable. || The *fix* is a ten-minute later start.", "WSN. | The festival made a *small* surplus. || That's the *first* in three years. || *Next* year we can book the headliner earlier.", "PREP. | Your son is *ready* for the senior band. || His reading has caught up with his sound. || He sight-read the Ellington cleanly on Friday. || He's *ready*."], "checks": ["Could I hear the structural words above the content?", "Did each section get its own breath?", "Did the last sentence land lower than the rest?"]}, {"id": "thought-2-d2", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Pick the shape fast", "how": "In the prep time, say aloud only the name of the structure you have chosen: PREP, TWO, WSN or PCF. Then answer using it. The choice matters more than the content.", "items": ["The sound system failed at the concert. What happened?", "Should Year 7s be allowed to choose their ensemble?", "What do you need from the executive this term?", "Why are string numbers down?", "How did the feeder band day go?", "Should the festival move to the winter?"], "prep": 3, "seconds": 35, "structure": "ANY", "checks": ["Did I name the structure before speaking?", "Did my answer follow the structure I named?", "Did I finish the shape rather than drift off it?", "Was the last sentence an action or a landing, not a new idea?"]}, {"id": "thought-2-d3", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Two things, under the clock", "how": "Announce \"two things\", deliver each with \"first\" and \"second\", then close with \"so\". If a third idea arrives, fold it into the \"so\" or let it go.", "items": ["A new staff member asks what they need to know about the Music department.", "The IMSS council asks what you would change about instrumental allocations.", "A parent asks what their child should do over the summer holidays.", "A panel asks what makes a good school jazz program.", "The principal asks what worries you about next year.", "A Year 11 asks how to prepare for their ATAR performance exam."], "structure": "TWO", "prep": 8, "seconds": 45, "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did I say \"two things\" and then deliver exactly two?", "Did I use \"first\", \"second\" and \"so\" aloud?", "Did I resist adding a third point?", "Did the \"so\" draw a conclusion rather than summarise?"]}, {"id": "thought-2-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Problem, cause, fix", "how": "One sentence for the problem, one for the cause, then spend the rest on the fix. Stop when the fix is clear.", "items": ["Concert Band 2 keeps losing its percussion section to sport.", "The festival's volunteer roster collapses every year in the final week.", "Parents are missing the email about the concert dress code.", "The instrument store is a mess and gear keeps going missing.", "Students arrive at Saturday rehearsals without their music.", "Council meetings run thirty minutes over."], "structure": "PCF", "prep": 10, "seconds": 50, "targets": {"fillers": 2, "wpm": [130, 165]}, "checks": ["Was the problem stated in one sentence?", "Did I spend more than half the time on the fix?", "Did I stay out of the history of the problem?", "Did I end on the fix, not the cause?"]}], "apply": {"source": "scenario", "task": "Read the scenario and decide in one second whether you are arguing, listing, reporting or fixing. Say the structure name aloud, then answer using it.", "seconds": 60, "checks": ["Did I choose the structure within a second?", "Did I complete every part of it?", "Would a listener be able to name the structure I used?"]}}, {"id": "thought-3", "title": "Signposting the route", "goal": "Use short spoken signposts so the room always knows where you are, and so do you.", "teach": {"body": "A reader can see the shape of a page: headings, paragraphs, the length of what is left. A listener cannot. Speech is linear and gone as soon as it is said, so the listener depends on you to announce the shape aloud. Signposts are the spoken version of headings: \"two things\", \"first\", \"the second thing\", \"so\". In rhetoric the opening announcement is called a partition, and classical speakers used it for the same reason you need it now.\n\nThe less obvious benefit is to you. A signpost is a commitment you have made out loud, and it pulls you back. Once you have said \"two things\", your mind keeps a running count whether you like it or not. When you drift into a tangent in the middle of the first point, the unspoken question \"where's the second thing?\" is already waiting. For an associative thinker, that is the single most useful property of signposting: it turns the room into an external memory.\n\nThere are four kinds. The preview: \"Two things.\" The marker: \"First.\" \"The second thing.\" The transition, which closes one section and opens another: \"That's the timing. Now the cost.\" And the landing: \"So.\" Each should be short, spoken slightly slower, and followed by a small pause. The pause is what makes it audible as structure rather than filler.\n\nUse \"the second thing\" rather than \"secondly\". It is plainer, and it restates the frame: it reminds the room that there are things being counted. Use \"so\" only for the landing, where it means \"therefore\". If \"so\" is also your filler at the start of sentences, you blunt your best signal; save it.\n\nWhat goes wrong. Over-signposting: \"I'm going to talk about three things, and the first thing I want to talk about is\" is a sentence that carries no content. Keep signposts to a word or two. Under-signposting: points that run together, so the room cannot tell whether you have moved on. And false signposts: announcing two and delivering three, or saying \"finally\" twice. Rehearsal with 28 teenagers punishes this immediately; they will tell you.\n\nHow to do it. Before you speak, decide the count. Say the preview. Say each marker as though it were a downbeat: clear, slightly stressed, then a breath. When you finish a point, close it with a transition before you open the next. Land with \"so\" and a sentence that ends lower than it began.", "model": "*Two* things before the concert. || *First*, | call time is *five-thirty*, | in the band room, | in blacks. || *The second thing*: | phones stay in your bag until the final bow. || *So* | five-thirty, | blacks, | phones away.", "mistake": "Okay so, a few things, so call time is five-thirty and also wear your blacks, oh and phones, so yeah, phones in bags, and five-thirty, so that's it.", "remember": ["Preview the count before the content.", "Say \"the second thing\", not \"secondly\".", "Pause after every signpost.", "Save \"so\" for the landing.", "Deliver exactly what you announced."]}, "drills": [{"id": "thought-3-d1", "type": "read", "title": "Signposts as downbeats", "how": "Read each item aloud. Slow down slightly on every signpost and give it a pause, as if it were a downbeat.", "items": ["*Two* things. || *First*, | the bus leaves at *seven*. || *The second thing*, | bring a packed lunch. || *So*, | seven, | lunch.", "That's the *budget*. || *Now* the timeline.", "*Two* reasons I'm saying yes. || *First*, | the kids are *ready*. || *The second thing*, | the venue is *free*. || *So*, | yes.", "That covers *strings*. || *Now* brass.", "*Two* things for the council tonight. || *First*, | the fee review. || *The second thing*, | next year's dates. || Let's start with the *fees*.", "*So*, | to land it: | the program *stays*, | and the fee *doesn't* change."], "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did each signpost have a pause after it?", "Did I avoid using \"so\" anywhere but the landing?", "Did the landing sentence end lower than it began?"]}, {"id": "thought-3-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Clutter to clean signposts", "how": "Say the cluttered signpost, then the clean one. Hear how much shorter the clean one is and how much clearer.", "items": ["What I'd like to do tonight is go through a couple of different things, the first of which is => Two things tonight. | First,", "Moving on now to the next item that I wanted to cover => The second thing.", "And then, sort of finally, I suppose, the last thing would be => So.", "Secondly, and this connects to what I said before in a way => The second thing.", "In terms of the other side of it, the money side of things => That's the plan. | Now the cost.", "So yeah, so basically, so in summary, so => So."], "checks": ["Was the clean version under five words?", "Did I pause after the clean version?", "Did the clean version make the count obvious?"]}, {"id": "thought-3-d3", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Count out loud", "how": "Preview the count, mark each point with \"first\" and \"the second thing\", use one transition sentence between them, and land with \"so\".", "items": ["Brief a relief teacher taking your Year 9 class tomorrow.", "Tell the jazz cellar audience what is coming in the second set.", "Give the IMSS council an update on the school's lesson numbers.", "Explain to a Year 7 parent how ensemble placement works.", "Set up a sectional for the saxophones.", "Open a department meeting with the agenda."], "structure": "TWO", "prep": 8, "seconds": 40, "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did I preview the count?", "Did I say \"the second thing\" aloud?", "Did I use a transition sentence between the points?", "Did \"so\" appear only at the landing?"]}, {"id": "thought-3-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Signposts as a lifeline", "how": "Use full signposting, but deliberately let one tangent in during the first point. Use the next signpost to pull yourself back.", "items": ["Tell the principal what you learned from this year's auditions.", "Explain to the AJEA board why the festival lost volunteers.", "Describe to a new parent what a week in the specialist program looks like.", "Report to council on the instrument hire scheme.", "Tell the staff what Music needs from them during concert week.", "Explain to a panel how you build a big band from beginners."], "structure": "TWO", "prep": 10, "seconds": 60, "targets": {"fillers": 3, "wpm": [130, 165]}, "checks": ["Did the signpost bring me back after the tangent?", "Did the room hear a clear break between the two points?", "Did I deliver exactly the number I previewed?", "Did I land with \"so\" and stop?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Read the passage and mark where its signposts are, or where they are missing. Then deliver it aloud, adding a preview, markers and a \"so\" landing where they belong, without adding any other words.", "seconds": 75, "checks": ["Did I add a preview before the first point?", "Did every new section begin with a marker?", "Did I land with \"so\" and a falling sentence?"]}}, {"id": "thought-4", "title": "Bracket and return", "goal": "Notice a tangent as it starts, park it aloud in one short phrase, and return to the line without losing the room.", "teach": {"body": "Tangents are not the enemy. The connection your mind made is often the most interesting thing in the room. The problem is that an unmanaged tangent takes the room with it and leaves no path back. The skill here is not suppression; it is bracketing: open the tangent deliberately, keep it short, close it out loud, and return to the line. Classical rhetoric called a deliberate departure a digression, and treated it as a device with a beginning and an end, not an accident.\n\nThere are three moves.\n\nNotice. Tangents announce themselves physically before they announce themselves logically. Your pace lifts, your pitch rises, your hands get busier, and you feel the small hit of a new idea. Learn your signs. In practice, the meter will show you: watch for the moment words per minute jumps mid-thought.\n\nPark. Say a short phrase that names the tangent and puts it aside: \"That's a separate conversation.\" \"Park that.\" \"I'll come back to that.\" \"Another day.\" Parking aloud does two things. It tells the room the departure was intentional, so they do not lose confidence in you. And it moves the idea out of your working memory and into the room's, where it stops nagging you. If the tangent matters, write a word on your page or ask the minute-taker to note it; then let it go.\n\nReturn. Use a return phrase that restates where you were: \"Back to the dates.\" \"So, the second thing.\" \"Where I was going: the fee.\" Restating is essential. Saying \"anyway\" returns you to nowhere; saying \"back to the fee\" returns you to the fee. This is where the signposts from the previous unit pay off: if you previewed \"two things\", the return can simply be \"the second thing\".\n\nWhat goes wrong. Apologising: \"Sorry, I'm going off on a tangent\" draws attention to the departure without closing it. Staying too long: a bracket should be one or two sentences, never a paragraph. And returning to the wrong place, or to a summary of everything, because you have lost where you were. That is why the return phrase names the point.\n\nWhen chairing, the same moves work on other people. \"That's worth its own item; let's minute it and come back to the fee\" is bracket and return applied to the room.", "model": "The concert moves to the *fourteenth*. || It's the same week as the *jazz* festival, | which, | actually, | is a whole conversation about the calendar. || *Park* that. || *Back* to the fourteenth: | it gives the bands a full extra week.", "mistake": "The concert moves to the fourteenth, which is the same week as the festival, and actually the whole calendar is a mess, we've been saying this for years, the clashes with sport too, anyway, sorry, where was I?", "remember": ["Notice the lift in pace and pitch.", "Park it aloud in three words.", "Return by naming the point.", "Never apologise for the tangent.", "One or two sentences, then close it."]}, "drills": [{"id": "thought-4-d1", "type": "read", "title": "Hear the bracket close", "how": "Read each item aloud. Drop your pitch on the parking phrase, pause, and stress the word that names the point you return to.", "items": ["The fee stays at *three hundred*. || There's a bigger question about hire, | but that's *another* day. || *Back* to the fee: | three hundred.", "Brass, | bar *forty*. || That reminds me of the Basie chart, | but *park* that. || Bar *forty*, | shorter notes.", "The bus leaves at *seven*. || Someone asked about the return trip, | I'll *come back* to that. || *Seven* o'clock, | out the front.", "We need two more *volunteers*. || The roster software is its own conversation. || *So*, | two volunteers, | by Friday.", "Your daughter is *ready* for senior band. || Her theory's a separate topic. || *Back* to the band: | she's *ready*.", "That's worth its own *item*. || Let's minute it. || *Back* to the budget."], "checks": ["Did the parking phrase fall in pitch?", "Did I pause after parking?", "Did the return name the point, not say \"anyway\"?"]}, {"id": "thought-4-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Lost to returned", "how": "Say the weak return, then the strong one. Hear how the strong version restates the line.", "items": ["Anyway, sorry, where was I? => Back to the dates.", "Sorry, I'm going off on a tangent here => That's a separate conversation.", "But that's not really relevant, I suppose, sorry => Park that.", "So, um, yeah, the thing I was saying => The second thing: the cost.", "I could talk about that all day, but anyway => Another day. | Back to the fee.", "Where did I start? Oh, right, the timetable, I think => So, the timetable."], "checks": ["Did the strong version name the point?", "Did I say the strong version without an apology?", "Did the strong version sound intentional?"]}, {"id": "thought-4-d3", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Take one tangent, return", "how": "Make your point, then deliberately take one tangent of no more than two sentences. Park it aloud and return by naming the point.", "items": ["Tell the principal why Concert Band 1 needs a Friday slot.", "Introduce the next tune at the jazz cellar.", "Explain to parents why the specialist program is a three-year commitment.", "Tell the IMSS council why lesson numbers dipped this term.", "Thank the festival volunteers at the closing night.", "Explain to a Year 8 class why you start rehearsal with long tones."], "structure": "PREP", "prep": 8, "seconds": 45, "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Was the tangent two sentences or fewer?", "Did I park it aloud with a short phrase?", "Did I return by naming the point?", "Did I finish the structure after returning?"]}, {"id": "thought-4-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Bracket while chairing", "how": "You are chairing. Speak for the member who goes off topic in one sentence, then bracket it for the room, minute it, and return the meeting to the item by name.", "items": ["Item: next year's concert dates. A member starts on the state of the hall's air-conditioning.", "Item: instrument hire fees. A member raises a complaint about a tutor.", "Item: festival ticket prices. A board member starts on the history of the venue.", "Item: Year 7 intake. A colleague raises their own timetable.", "Item: Nannup logistics. A parent asks about next year's trip.", "Item: the budget. A member wants to revisit last month's minutes."], "structure": "ABA", "prep": 8, "seconds": 30, "targets": {"fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Did I acknowledge the point without engaging with it?", "Did I give it a place (minuted, next item, another meeting)?", "Did I return the room to the item by name?", "Did I sound settled rather than irritated?"]}], "apply": {"source": "story", "task": "Read the story, then retell it aloud. At one point, take a deliberate tangent about something it reminds you of, park it in one phrase, and return to the story by naming where you were.", "seconds": 90, "checks": ["Did the tangent stay under two sentences?", "Did I park it without apologising?", "Did I return to the exact point in the story?", "Did the retelling still reach its ending?"]}}, {"id": "thought-5", "title": "Anchors, not a script", "goal": "Deliver a prepared talk from three anchors and two memorised sentences, without notes and without losing the order.", "teach": {"body": "A memorised script is fragile. It is stored as a chain: each sentence cues the next. Lose one link, through a cough, a late arrival, a phone in the second row, and the chain breaks. You stand there searching for the exact next word, and the room watches you search. Reading a script is safer but costs you the room: your eyes, your timing, your conversational pitch.\n\nAnchors are sturdier. An anchor is a short phrase or image that holds one section of the talk. You memorise the anchors and their order, not the words between them. Under each anchor you know what you want to say, and you say it fresh each time. If you lose a sentence, you have not lost the talk; you go to the next anchor.\n\nThree anchors is the working number. Three fits comfortably in a stressed working memory, it gives a talk a beginning, middle and end, and if you lose one you can still find the other two. For a concert introduction: the welcome, the program, the thanks. For a council report: the result, what it means, what we decide tonight.\n\nMemorise two things word for word: the first sentence and the last. The serial position effect, one of the oldest findings in memory research, shows that people remember beginnings and endings best. Your audience will too. And the opening sentence is where nerves peak; having it ready means your mouth knows what to do while your body settles. The closing sentence gives you a place to land, so you never trail off into \"so, yeah, that's it\".\n\nThe method of loci makes the order hard to lose. It is the ancient memory palace, attributed to Simonides, and it is still the method memory champions use. Pick a route you know physically: your front door, your hallway, your kitchen. Place one anchor at each spot as a vivid image. To recall the talk, walk the route in your mind. Spatial memory is strong, and it gives the order for free. For a three-anchor talk, three rooms of your house is enough.\n\nWhat goes wrong. Writing the talk in full and then trying to remember it: you will remember the script, not the anchors. Too many anchors: if there are seven, you are back to a script. Rehearsing silently: say it aloud, every time, differently. The variation is the point; it proves you are speaking from the anchors.\n\nHow to do it. Write the opening sentence, three anchors, and the closing sentence. Nothing else. Walk the loci. Speak it three times, different words each time.", "model": "*Good* evening, | and *welcome* to the winter concert. || Tonight you'll hear *three* bands, | from Year 7s in their *first* concert | to seniors in their *last*. || None of it happens without the parents who drive, | wait and pay. || *Enjoy* the music, | and thank you for being *here*.", "mistake": "Good evening and welcome to the, um, to the annual winter concert which tonight features, hang on, features our, sorry, I had this written down.", "remember": ["Three anchors, in order.", "Memorise the first and last sentence exactly.", "Say the middle fresh every time.", "Place each anchor in a room you know.", "Rehearse aloud, never silently."]}, "drills": [{"id": "thought-5-d1", "type": "repeat", "title": "Lock the first sentence", "how": "Say each opening sentence four times: once slowly, once at speaking pace, once with a different stressed word, once looking away from the phone. By the fourth, it should come without effort.", "items": ["Good evening, and welcome to the winter concert.", "Thank you all for coming tonight; I'll keep this brief.", "I'd like to open the meeting and welcome our two new members.", "This next tune is one of Mingus's, and it was written for a friend.", "Welcome to Mount Lawley, and to the specialist music program.", "Good morning, everyone; I'm here with one request."], "checks": ["Could I say it the fourth time without looking?", "Did the stress change make it sound different, not wrong?", "Did the last word fall in pitch every time?"]}, {"id": "thought-5-d2", "type": "recall", "title": "Three anchors, short", "how": "Read the anchors in the prep time, place each one in a room of your house, then speak the talk from memory in order.", "items": ["Welcome / the three bands tonight / thank the parents", "The festival result / why the surplus matters / what we decide tonight", "Who I am / what the program asks / what your child gets", "The tune / the composer's story / the solo order", "The problem with Saturdays / the cause / the new arrangement", "Congratulations / what the award recognises / one thing about the recipient"], "prep": 10, "seconds": 45, "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did I deliver all three anchors in order?", "Did I use different words from the prompt?", "Did I recover the next anchor without stopping?"]}, {"id": "thought-5-d3", "type": "recall", "title": "Fixed ends, fresh middle", "how": "The first and last items are word-for-word sentences; say them exactly. The middle three are anchors; say them fresh.", "items": ["Good evening, and welcome to the winter concert. / Concert Band 2's first year / the jazz band's festival result / the seniors' farewell / Enjoy the music, and thank you for being here.", "Thank you for coming tonight. / what the specialist program is / what three years gives a child / how auditions work / We look forward to meeting your child.", "I'll open with the treasurer's report. / income this year / the one cost that surprised us / what I'm recommending / I'll take questions now.", "This is our last tune tonight. / who wrote it / why we play it last / the band / Thank you, and goodnight.", "I'd like to say a few words about Marion. / how we met / what she gave the program / what we will miss / Thank you, Marion."], "prep": 15, "seconds": 75, "targets": {"fillers": 2, "wpm": [130, 160]}, "checks": ["Was the opening sentence word for word?", "Was the closing sentence word for word?", "Did the middle anchors come out in order?", "Did I stop after the closing sentence?"]}, {"id": "thought-5-d4", "type": "recall", "title": "Recall under disruption", "how": "Speak the talk from anchors. After the first anchor, stop, count three seconds silently as if interrupted, then pick up at the second anchor without going back.", "items": ["Welcome / a year of firsts / the students who led it / the people behind them / goodnight", "The audition numbers / what changed in the format / the waiting list / the decision for council", "The award / the student's first year / the moment it turned / why tonight", "The venue / the band / the next three tunes / the bar is open", "The proposal / the cost / the risk / the vote"], "prep": 15, "seconds": 75, "targets": {"fillers": 3, "pauses": 1}, "checks": ["Did I resume at the next anchor rather than repeat?", "Did the interruption leave no filler behind it?", "Did all anchors appear in order?", "Did I land the final anchor cleanly?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Read the passage once. Reduce it to an opening sentence, three anchors and a closing sentence, place the anchors along a route in your house, then deliver it without the text.", "seconds": 75, "checks": ["Did I get the opening and closing sentences exactly?", "Did all three anchors appear in the right order?", "Did I speak the middle in my own words?", "Did I avoid looking back at the text?"]}}, {"id": "thought-6", "title": "The question you didn't expect", "goal": "Meet an unexpected question by buying two honest seconds, answering with a clear structure, and stopping.", "teach": {"body": "The unexpected question is where a fast mind is most at risk. Before the question is finished you have five possible answers, three qualifications, and a memory of a related incident from 2014. The temptation is to start talking before you have chosen, because silence feels like failure. It is not. The room reads a short, composed pause as thought. It reads instant, rushing speech as nerves.\n\nBuy two seconds honestly. You do not need to fake anything. You can simply pause, look at the person, and breathe out. Or use a short line that is true: \"Good question.\" only if it is one; \"Let me think about that.\"; \"There are two parts to that.\" Repeating or restating the question is also honest, and it checks you have understood it: \"You're asking whether the fee will rise next year.\" What you must not do is fill the two seconds with \"um, so, I mean, well, look\". That is time bought dishonestly, and it shows on the meter.\n\nUse those seconds for one decision: what is the answer, in one sentence? Then reach for a structure. For a hostile, loaded or off-topic question, use ABA: acknowledge, bridge, answer. Acknowledge the concern in a sentence without accepting its premise (\"I understand fees are a worry for families\"). Bridge with a short phrase (\"what I can tell you is\"). Answer with the point you actually stand behind. ABA comes from media training, and it is often taught as a way to dodge. Used well, it is the reverse: it lets you answer honestly without being steered into answering a different question.\n\nFor a fair question you simply did not expect, use the headline from the first unit, then PREP or TWO if there is more to say.\n\nThen stop. This is the hardest part. When the answer is done, close your mouth and hold eye contact. The silence after an answer is not yours to fill. People who keep talking after the answer tend to add qualifications that weaken it, or new material that invites a second question. If you are chairing, the stop is your cue to move: \"Next question.\" At a panel, a short nod and a look back to the moderator does the same.\n\nWhat goes wrong. Answering a question you wished they had asked. Apologising for not knowing: if you do not know, say \"I don't know; I'll find out by Friday\" and stop. And the long run-up: three sentences of context before the answer arrives.", "model": "|| You're asking whether the fee will *rise*. || I understand that's a worry for families. || What I can tell you is | the council has *frozen* it for next year. || That's the decision. ||", "mistake": "Um, so, well, look, that's a really good question, and I think, I mean, the thing about fees is that there's a lot of history, so in 2019 we had a review, and, sorry, what was the question again?", "remember": ["Pause before answering; silence reads as thought.", "Restate the question if it buys clarity.", "Find the one-sentence answer first.", "Hostile or loaded: acknowledge, bridge, answer.", "Answer, then stop and hold the look."]}, "drills": [{"id": "thought-6-d1", "type": "hold", "title": "Two seconds, then answer", "how": "Read each question to yourself, hold the silence without moving, then say the one-sentence answer aloud and hold silence again after it.", "items": ["Why is your department asking for more money than drama?", "Is the festival financially sustainable?", "Why didn't my son get into the senior band?", "What's your plan if the funding is cut?", "Do you think music should be compulsory?", "What's the biggest weakness in your program?"], "hold": 2, "targets": {"fillers": 0, "pauses": 2}, "checks": ["Did I hold the silence without a filler?", "Was my answer one sentence?", "Did I hold silence after the answer rather than add to it?"]}, {"id": "thought-6-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Dishonest time, honest time", "how": "Say the weak opening, then the strong one. Hear the difference between filling time and buying it.", "items": ["Um, so, yeah, look, that's a really good question, I mean => Let me think about that.", "Sorry, I'm not sure, I mean I probably should know this => I don't know. | I'll find out by Friday.", "Well, there's a lot to that, and it's complicated, and => There are two parts to that.", "Oh, wow, okay, um, I wasn't expecting that one => You're asking whether we'll keep the program.", "Right, so, I guess what I'd say is, sort of => The short answer is no.", "Hmm, yeah, look, I hear you, I really do, but => I understand the concern. | Here's what I can tell you."], "checks": ["Did the strong version contain no fillers?", "Did the strong version sound calm rather than defensive?", "Was the strong version honest about what I know?"]}, {"id": "thought-6-d3", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Acknowledge, bridge, answer", "how": "Acknowledge the concern in one sentence without agreeing with its premise, bridge in a short phrase, give the answer you stand behind, then stop.", "items": ["A parent: isn't the three-year commitment just a way to keep numbers up?", "A council member: why should the school fund an elite program for a few kids?", "A journalist: is it true the festival nearly went broke?", "A colleague in a staff meeting: doesn't Music get special treatment on excursions?", "A parent: my child says rehearsals are a waste of time. Are they?", "A panel host: aren't school jazz programs a bit old-fashioned?"], "structure": "ABA", "prep": 5, "seconds": 30, "targets": {"fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Did I acknowledge without accepting the premise?", "Was the bridge short and audible?", "Did I answer the real question, not a safer one?", "Did I stop after the answer?"]}, {"id": "thought-6-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Cold questions, clean stops", "how": "Take the question cold. Buy two seconds honestly, choose ABA if it is loaded or a headline with PREP if it is fair, answer, then stop and hold the look for two seconds.", "items": ["The principal, in the corridor: what would you change if you ran the arts faculty?", "A Year 12, after rehearsal: do you think I'm good enough to study music?", "A council member: why are you also on the festival board? Isn't that a conflict?", "An audience member at the cellar: what's the hardest thing about playing bass?", "A parent at an award night: why did my daughter not get an award?", "A panel: what's the one thing schools get wrong about music?"], "prep": 2, "seconds": 40, "structure": "ABA", "targets": {"fillers": 1, "pauses": 2}, "checks": ["Did I pause before the first word?", "Did I pick the right structure for the question?", "Did I stop as soon as the answer was complete?", "Did I hold eye contact in the silence after?"]}], "apply": {"source": "scenario", "task": "Read the scenario as if the question has just been asked. Pause for two seconds, answer aloud using ABA or a headline, then stop. Do it again, cutting anything after the answer.", "seconds": 40, "structure": "ABA", "checks": ["Did I buy time without fillers?", "Did the answer arrive in my first two sentences?", "Did I stop and stay silent at the end?"]}}]}, {"strand": "Words", "intro": "This strand trains the words themselves: what to cut, what to choose, what to say when the moment is hard, and which forms make a line stay in the room. Your voice and presence already carry authority. Fillers, hedges and vague nouns quietly give it away, and these six units take that leak out, one habit at a time.", "units": [{"id": "words-1", "title": "Pause Instead of Filler", "goal": "Replace um, like, you know, sort of, basically and the launching 'so' with a clean silent pause, including under pressure.", "teach": {"body": "A filler is the sound of your speech planner holding the floor while it fetches the next phrase. Psycholinguists, Herbert Clark among them, have shown that um and uh are not random noise: speakers use them to signal that a delay is coming, um for a longer one. In conversation that signal does a job. It stops the other person cutting in. In a room you are chairing, conducting or addressing from a mic, nobody is going to cut in. You already hold the floor. The filler is doing a job that does not need doing, and the room hears it as uncertainty.\n\nFillers come in three families. Planning fillers (um, uh, er) fill the gap while you think. Social fillers (like, you know, right) ask the listener to nod along. Softeners (sort of, basically, kind of) blur a claim you have not committed to; unit 2 deals with those properly. The launching 'so' at the start of an answer is a fourth case: a run-up noise before the first real word.\n\nThey cluster in predictable places: the first word of an answer, the seam between two points, and just before a word you are still choosing. They also breed in run-on sentences. 'And, um, so, and then' is what happens when a sentence never ends. Finish the sentence and the filler has nowhere to attach.\n\nThe replacement is a pause, and the pause has a physical shape. At the end of the phrase, close your lips. Breathe in through your nose. Then speak. You cannot vocalise on a nasal in-breath, so the place where the um wanted to go becomes air. To you, a one-second pause feels like three. To the room it reads as thought, and it gives them time to absorb the sentence you just finished. You know this from the bandstand: the rest is part of the line, and a bassist who fills every beat with ghost notes has no groove.\n\nFor the opening 'so', start on the noun. Not 'So, the camp', but 'The camp'. Not 'So, basically, what happened was', but 'Here is what happened.'\n\nDo not aim at zero on day one. Aim at hearing them. When the meter catches one, stop, close your lips, breathe, and say the sentence again clean. That repetition is the training. After a fortnight the nasal breath starts arriving on its own at the seams.", "model": "Good question. || The band rehearses *twice* a week, | Tuesday and Thursday mornings. || Missing one is fine. | Missing three in a row | is a *conversation*. || The part your child will notice first | is the *sound* of a full section. || That happens in about *six* weeks.", "mistake": "So, um, basically the band sort of rehearses, like, twice a week, you know, Tuesdays and, um, Thursdays, and so if they miss, like, a few, that's, um, kind of a problem.", "remember": ["Close your lips at the end of every phrase.", "Breathe in through the nose where the um wants to go.", "Start on the noun, never on 'so'.", "Finish the sentence instead of chaining with 'and'.", "When you catch a filler, say the sentence again clean."]}, "drills": [{"id": "words-1-d1", "type": "read", "title": "Read the Rests", "how": "Read each item aloud, closing your lips and taking a nasal breath at every | and holding a beat at every ||. Nothing voiced in the gaps.", "items": ["Welcome to the Year 9 *concert*. || We have three bands tonight | and one *very* nervous trumpet section.", "The first item is the *budget*. || We are four hundred dollars *under*. | That is good news.", "Cases on the *left*. | Stands up. || Concert *B-flat*, | on my count.", "This next tune is by *Kenny Wheeler*. || It sits in three, | but it doesn't *feel* like it.", "I've read the report. || Two things stood *out*. | The first is attendance.", "Thank you for coming. || Drive home *safely*, | and we'll see the band on *Monday*."], "targets": {"fillers": 0, "pauses": 3}, "checks": ["Was every gap silent, with no voiced sound at all?", "Did you breathe in through your nose at each |?", "Did each || last a full beat before you went on?", "Did the meter show zero fillers?"]}, {"id": "words-1-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Strip the Filler", "how": "Say the weak line as written, fillers and all, then the strong line with a closed-lip pause wherever a filler stood. Listen to which one sounds like the person in charge.", "items": ["So, um, the main thing is attendance. => The main thing | is *attendance*.", "It's, like, basically a three-year commitment, you know? => It's a *three-year* commitment.", "Um, so, the next tune is, sort of, a ballad. => The next tune | is a *ballad*.", "So, what happened was, um, the bus was late. => The bus was *late*. || Here's what happened next.", "I've, um, looked at the, uh, the numbers and, like, they're fine. => I've looked at the numbers. || They're *fine*.", "So basically we need, um, a decision tonight. => We need a decision | *tonight*."], "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did the strong line start on a content word rather than 'so'?", "Was the pause in the strong line silent?", "Did the strong line end lower than it began?", "Could you hear the drop in authority in the weak version?"]}, {"id": "words-1-d3", "type": "hold", "title": "Silence at the Seams", "how": "Say each line, then hold silence for the full count with lips closed before the next line. The lines run together as one answer, so the silences sit exactly where fillers usually breed.", "items": ["The short answer is *yes*.", "Your daughter can change instruments at the end of Year 7.", "The reason we wait is *reading*.", "By then she can read on one instrument, and that transfers.", "If she changes earlier, she starts reading from *zero* again.", "So the end of the year | is the right time."], "hold": 2, "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did you hold every silence for the full two seconds?", "Were your lips closed during each hold?", "Did any um or so sneak in at the start of a line?", "Did the silence feel shorter by the last line than the first?"]}, {"id": "words-1-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Clean Under Questioning", "how": "Answer each question as if a parent or staff member has just asked it. Begin on a content word, use a nasal breath at every seam, and stop when the point is made.", "items": ["Why does my son have to do theory if he just wants to play?", "What is the Music department's biggest need next year?", "Why did the concert start twenty minutes late?", "What makes a good bass line?", "Why should a Year 10 student stay in band when the workload rises?", "What did the band do best this term?"], "seconds": 60, "prep": 5, "structure": "PREP", "targets": {"fillers": 2, "pauses": 6}, "checks": ["Was your first word a content word, not 'so' or 'um'?", "Did the meter show two fillers or fewer?", "Did you finish sentences rather than chain them with 'and'?", "Did you stop after restating the point?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Read the passage aloud once from the page. Then deliver it from memory, closing your lips and breathing through the nose at every seam where you feel an um arrive.", "seconds": 60, "checks": ["Did the from-memory version have zero fillers?", "Did you begin without 'so' or a throat-clear?", "Were the pauses longest at the seams between ideas?", "Did you end on a finished sentence and silence?"]}}, {"id": "words-2", "title": "The Strong Version", "goal": "State claims and requests without hedges, apologies or checking tags, keeping a single exact qualifier only where the doubt is real.", "teach": {"body": "A hedge is a word that lowers your commitment to what you are saying. There are four common kinds. Minimisers shrink the request: just, quickly, a bit, only. Epistemic hedges blur the claim: I think, maybe, probably, I guess, sort of. Apologies ask permission to exist: sorry, sorry to be a pain. Checking tags hand the verdict to the listener: does that make sense, if that's okay, right?\n\nThe linguist Robin Lakoff grouped these features in the 1970s, and courtroom research by William O'Barr and colleagues later found that witnesses who used them were judged less credible and less competent, whoever they were. The listener does not hear politeness. They hear that you are not sure, and they discount the content by the same amount.\n\nA precise mind hedges for an honourable reason: it knows the exceptions. The fix is not to pretend to certainty you do not have. It is to change the form of the doubt. A hedge spreads doubt across the whole sentence. A qualifier puts it exactly where it belongs. 'I think we might be able to move sectionals' becomes 'Sectionals move to Wednesday. The one open question is the hall booking, and I'll know by Friday.' The first is fog. The second is a claim plus a measured unknown, and it is more honest.\n\nSome practical rules. 'Just' can almost always be deleted with no loss of meaning; test it. 'I think' before a recommendation should become the reason: not 'I think we should cap the band at forty', but 'Cap the band at forty. Above that, I can't hear individual players.' Apologise once, for something real, then fix it; reflexive sorries spend the apology you might need later. Replace 'does that make sense?' with 'Questions?' or with nothing. If they are confused they will tell you.\n\nHedging has a sound as well as a vocabulary. It usually comes with a rising pitch at the end of the phrase, so the statement arrives as a question. The meter tracks that rise. The strong version ends lower than it began: the pitch steps down onto the last stressed word and stops. Say the sentence, land it, close your mouth. The silence after a strong statement is where the room accepts it.", "model": "We're moving sectionals to *Wednesday*. || The reason is the *hall*: | it's free on Wednesdays and on no other day. || If that clashes with one of your classes, | tell me *today*, | and I'll fix it individually. || Questions?", "mistake": "Sorry, I just wanted to maybe raise something, I think we might possibly move sectionals to Wednesday? If that's okay with everyone? Does that make sense?", "remember": ["Delete 'just'; the sentence survives.", "Replace 'I think' with the reason.", "Apologise once, for something real, then fix it.", "Put the doubt in one exact place.", "End on a falling full stop, not a question."]}, "drills": [{"id": "words-2-d1", "type": "swap", "title": "Delete the Hedge", "how": "Say the hedged line, then the strong line. Make the strong line fall in pitch onto its last stressed word and stop.", "items": ["I just wanted to quickly check if maybe the hall is free? => Is the hall free on *Thursday*?", "I think we should probably cap the band at forty. => Cap the band at *forty*. || Above that I can't hear individuals.", "Sorry, can I just add something? => One more *point*.", "It's sort of a really important concert for us, I guess. => This is our most important concert of the *year*.", "Does that make sense? => *Questions*?", "I was maybe wondering if I could possibly have a minute? => I need *five* minutes with you this week."], "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did the strong line contain no just, maybe, sorry or I think?", "Did the strong line end lower than it began?", "Was the strong line shorter than the weak one?", "Did you stop dead after the strong line?"]}, {"id": "words-2-d2", "type": "repeat", "title": "Land the Statement", "how": "Say each statement three times. First with a rising end, as if asking permission. Second flat. Third stepping down in pitch onto the starred word and stopping.", "items": ["The motion is *carried*.", "Rehearsal starts at *seven-thirty*.", "Your son is ready for the *senior* band.", "We can't fund a second *tour* this year.", "The next tune is *Footprints*.", "I'm recommending we *keep* the fee as it is."], "checks": ["Could you hear three distinct endings?", "On the third pass, did the pitch fall onto the starred word?", "Did the meter show no end-of-phrase rise on the third pass?", "Did the third version sound like a decision rather than a suggestion?"]}, {"id": "words-2-d3", "type": "impromptu", "title": "The Clean Request", "how": "Make each request in one sentence plus one reason. No apology, no minimiser, no checking tag. Then stop.", "items": ["Ask the principal for a second music room on Tuesdays.", "Ask a colleague to stop booking the performing arts centre without telling you.", "Ask the festival committee for an extra fifty dollars per student for the youth stage.", "Ask a student to stop playing during your instructions.", "Ask a parent to collect their child on time after evening rehearsals.", "Ask the council to move the next meeting forward a week."], "seconds": 30, "prep": 5, "structure": "ONE", "targets": {"fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Did you avoid sorry, just and maybe completely?", "Was the request in your first sentence?", "Did you give one reason rather than three?", "Did you stop without adding 'if that's okay'?"]}, {"id": "words-2-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Calibrated Certainty", "how": "Give a recommendation on each issue. State it plainly, then name exactly one real uncertainty and when it will be resolved. No other hedges.", "items": ["Should the festival add a second night next year?", "Should Year 7 auditions move earlier in the year?", "Should the junior band perform at the end-of-year assembly?", "Should the department buy a new double bass or repair the old one?", "Should students be allowed to drop theory in Year 10?", "Should the school council support a fee increase for instrumental lessons?"], "seconds": 60, "prep": 10, "structure": "PREP", "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did you state the recommendation in the first sentence?", "Did you name one specific unknown, not a general fog?", "Did you say when that unknown will be resolved?", "Did every sentence end falling rather than rising?"]}], "apply": {"source": "pair", "task": "Say the weak version, then the strong version. Then say two more strong versions of your own on the same content, each shorter than the last.", "seconds": 45, "checks": ["Did each of your versions remove every hedge from the weak one?", "Was your shortest version still complete and courteous?", "Did each version end on a falling full stop?"]}}, {"id": "words-3", "title": "The Precise Word", "goal": "Choose the concrete noun, the actual number and the verb that does the work, in plain English, while speaking without a script.", "teach": {"body": "S. I. Hayakawa described language as a ladder of abstraction. At the bottom is the thing you can point at: Charlie's cracked trombone slide. Higher up: a damaged instrument, then equipment, then resources. Each rung up covers more cases and shows less. Meetings drift up the ladder because abstraction feels safe and sounds official. Rooms remember the bottom rungs. The concreteness effect is one of the steadiest findings in memory research: concrete words, the ones that produce an image, are recalled better than abstract ones. 'We need resources' is gone by the next agenda item. 'Six of our ten trombones have dented slides' is still in the room next month.\n\nThe number beats 'a lot'. 'Lots of students' could be eight or eighty. Say 'forty-one'. For the ear, round sensibly: 'nearly half' lands better than 'forty-seven point three per cent', and one number per sentence is about the limit a listener can hold. Give the number a comparison so it means something: 'forty-one, up from twenty-six'.\n\nThe verb should do the work. Official English hides verbs inside nouns, which linguists call nominalisation: 'make a decision', 'conduct a review', 'provide support to', 'have a discussion about'. Each one is a weak verb dragging a noun. Pull the verb back out: decide, review, support, discuss. The sentence gets shorter and someone is now doing something.\n\nPlain English is the same discipline at word level. Orwell's rules still hold: never use a long word where a short one will do; if a word can be cut, cut it. Use, not utilise. Start, not commence. Help, not facilitate. Management-speak (going forward, learnings, leverage, touch base) is abstraction with a lanyard on. It tells the room you are reciting rather than thinking.\n\nThere is a delivery dividend. English puts its natural stress near the end of a clause, a pattern grammarians call end focus. Put the precise word last and the stress falls on it without effort: not 'Forty-one students is the number who auditioned', but 'The number who auditioned was forty-one.' Precise words need less vocal help. The content carries the weight, so you can speak more quietly and still be heard.\n\nUnder pressure, when you hear yourself reach for 'things' or 'stuff' or 'a lot', stop for a beat and go one rung down.", "model": "Last year | *thirty-eight* students sat the instrumental exams. || *Thirty-one* passed with merit. || The seven who didn't | had each missed more than *six* lessons. || So the fix is not more repertoire. || It's *attendance*.", "mistake": "Engagement with the instrumental program has been really strong across a lot of cohorts, and outcomes have basically been positive overall, going forward.", "remember": ["Name the thing, not the category.", "Say the number, rounded for the ear.", "Pull the verb out of the noun.", "Short plain word over long official one.", "Put the precise word last."]}, "drills": [{"id": "words-3-d1", "type": "swap", "title": "Free the Verb", "how": "Say the nominalised version, then the version with the verb freed. Notice who is doing what in the second.", "items": ["We made a decision to conduct a review of the timetable. => We *reviewed* the timetable.", "The committee will undertake a discussion about the budget. => The committee will *discuss* the budget.", "I'll provide support to the new strings teacher. => I'll *support* the new strings teacher.", "There was a failure to achieve a quorum. => We didn't reach a *quorum*.", "We need to facilitate the commencement of rehearsals. => Rehearsals *start* on Monday.", "Students demonstrated an improvement in their sight-reading. => The students *read* better."], "checks": ["Did each strong line contain a verb doing real work?", "Was each strong line shorter than the weak one?", "Could you name who is doing the action in each strong line?", "Did the stress fall on the freed verb or the final word?"]}, {"id": "words-3-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "One Rung Down", "how": "Say the abstract line, then the concrete one. Let the concrete line end on the image or the number.", "items": ["We have significant resourcing challenges. => Six of our ten trombones have *dented slides*.", "A lot of students came to auditions. => *Forty-one* students auditioned, | up from *twenty-six*.", "The concert was well received. => The audience stood for the *encore*.", "There are some behavioural issues in the ensemble. => Three students talk through every *count-in*.", "The festival had a strong financial outcome. => The festival made *two thousand dollars*.", "The students are very engaged. => They arrive at seven and *ask* to start early."], "checks": ["Did each concrete line produce an image or a number?", "Did each concrete line end on its precise word?", "Did you round any number for the ear?", "Did the concrete line sound more confident without extra effort?"]}, {"id": "words-3-d3", "type": "recall", "title": "Concrete Anchors", "how": "Read the anchors during prep, then speak the short talk in order once they disappear. Every anchor must come out as its exact noun or number, never a vaguer substitute.", "items": ["Twenty-eight students / four rehearsals cancelled / the hall flooded in March / concert still sold out", "New double bass / three thousand two hundred dollars / old one cracked at the neck / students waiting since February", "Festival youth stage / six school bands / ninety minutes / two hundred families in the audience", "Year 7 intake / sixty-two auditions / thirty-four places / results by the end of term", "Junior big band / Blue Bossa / first time playing a solo / eleven of fourteen students had a go"], "seconds": 45, "prep": 15, "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did every number come out exactly as written?", "Did you avoid 'a lot', 'things' and 'stuff' entirely?", "Did the anchors come out in order?", "Did the last sentence end on a concrete word?"]}, {"id": "words-3-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Describe It Exactly", "how": "Speak on each topic using at least three concrete details and at least one number with a comparison. No abstract noun unless you immediately make it concrete.", "items": ["Describe last week's rehearsal to a colleague who wasn't there.", "Tell the council what the music program actually costs per student.", "Describe the sound of your bass to someone who has never heard it.", "Explain to parents what a good practice session looks like.", "Describe the best gig you played this year.", "Tell the principal what the department needs most, and why."], "seconds": 60, "prep": 10, "structure": "WSN", "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did you give at least three details someone could picture?", "Did you use at least one number with a comparison?", "Did you catch yourself on any abstract word and go one rung down?", "Did you use no management-speak at all?"]}], "apply": {"source": "fact", "task": "Read the fact. State it in one sentence for parents and one sentence for the council, using the number once in each, and put it last in the sentence.", "seconds": 30, "checks": ["Did the number land at the end of each sentence?", "Did you round it sensibly for the ear?", "Did each sentence use plain words for that room?"]}}, {"id": "words-4", "title": "Words for the Room", "goal": "Shift vocabulary, formality and sentence length to suit each of his rooms, and decide in the moment whether a big word helps or hurts.", "teach": {"body": "Register is the variety of language a situation calls for. The linguist Michael Halliday broke it into three parts: field (what you are talking about), tenor (who you are talking to and your relationship with them) and mode (spoken or written, live or prepared). Change any one and the right words change. Speakers who sound wrong in a room are usually right on field and wrong on tenor: correct content, wrong relationship.\n\nYour rooms each have a register. Council is formal and procedural: 'I move that', 'through the chair', 'the motion is carried'. Its formality is a tool; it keeps disagreement impersonal. Staff meetings are collegial and efficient, and shared jargon is fine because everyone holds the key. Parents need warmth, no acronyms and outcomes for their own child: not 'ATAR pathway', but 'the course that counts towards university entry'. Students need short sentences, imperatives, counts and humour that lands on the situation, never on a person. The bandstand needs brevity and ease: tune, composer, players, done, ideally under thirty seconds, because they came for the music. A ceremony allows the most lift: longer sentences, slower pace, some ornament, every name given in full and pronounced correctly.\n\nA big word helps in two cases. When it is the exact word and the room owns it: 'quorum' to a council, 'hemiola' to a jazz crowd that knows what they're hearing. And when the occasion asks for elevation: at a ceremony, 'we honour' is right where 'we want to say thanks' would shrink the moment. A big word hurts when a plainer one is just as exact, when the room does not own it, or when its real purpose is to show that you know it. The test is simple: would this room use this word themselves? If not, translate it or explain it in the same breath.\n\nThere are two ways to miss. Under-shifting is jargon leaking into the wrong tenor: talking to parents as if they were a curriculum committee. Over-shifting is worse: borrowed slang with teenagers, or a ceremonial voice in a staff meeting. Both sound like a performance. The aim is the same person in different clothes. Your values, your humour and your judgment stay fixed. Vocabulary, sentence length and formality move.\n\nPhysically, register also lives in pace and pitch range. Ceremony is slower with longer phrases. Rehearsal is quicker, with crisp consonants. The bandstand mic sits close, so you can drop to a conversational volume and let the system do the work.", "model": "Your child will learn *two* things in band this year. || The first is to read music *fluently*. || The second is to play with thirty other people | without being told when to come *in*. || The second one | takes *longer*. || It's also the one they'll *keep*.", "mistake": "This year's pedagogical focus is on developing notational literacy and ensemble autonomy across the cohort, in line with the SCSA syllabus outcomes.", "remember": ["Ask: would this room say this word?", "Explain every acronym or drop it.", "Shortest sentences for students, longest at ceremonies.", "Use the big word only when it is exact.", "Change the clothes, not the person."]}, "drills": [{"id": "words-4-d1", "type": "swap", "title": "Translate the Jargon", "how": "Say the staffroom version, then the version for the room named in brackets. Keep the meaning exact while changing the words.", "items": ["(Parents) Students on the ATAR pathway need consistent instrumental tuition. => If your child wants Music to count for *university*, | they need a lesson every *week*.", "(Students) We're going to address intonation issues in the upper woodwinds. => Flutes and clarinets, | you're *sharp*. || Let's fix it.", "(Bandstand) This composition utilises a modal framework derived from Dorian. => This one's by *Wayne Shorter*. || It floats. || Enjoy it.", "(Council) The kids reckon the fee hike's a bit much. => Students have raised concerns that the proposed fee is *unaffordable*.", "(Ceremony) Thanks heaps to Jane for all her stuff over the years. => Tonight we *honour* Jane Ellis, | for eighteen years of service to this school.", "(Parents) Your son has demonstrated deficits in rhythmic subdivision. => Your son is rushing the *off-beats*. || It's very fixable."], "checks": ["Did the second version use words that room would use itself?", "Was the meaning unchanged between the two?", "Did any acronym survive into the parent or student version?", "Did you still sound like yourself in both?"]}, {"id": "words-4-d2", "type": "repeat", "title": "One Message, Three Rooms", "how": "Say each message three times: for the council, then for parents, then for students. Change the vocabulary, sentence length and pace each time; keep the facts identical.", "items": ["The end-of-year concert is moving from the hall to the performing arts centre.", "Instrumental lesson fees are rising by forty dollars a term.", "The band has been invited to play at the jazz festival.", "Rehearsals will be cancelled for two weeks while the music room is repaired.", "We're introducing a practice log that students fill in every week.", "Two senior students have been selected for the state honour band."], "checks": ["Were the facts identical across all three versions?", "Were the student sentences the shortest?", "Did the council version use procedural language naturally?", "Did the parent version state what it means for their child?"]}, {"id": "words-4-d3", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Read the Room Fast", "how": "Speak to the room named in each item, using its register from the first word. Keep it to one point and stop.", "items": ["At the jazz venue mic: introduce the next tune and the drummer.", "To the Year 8 band: tell them the run-through was sloppy and why.", "To the school council: open the item on the music fee.", "To a parent evening: explain why Year 7 auditions happen.", "At an award ceremony: introduce the student receiving the music prize.", "To the staff meeting: ask for help supervising the concert."], "seconds": 30, "prep": 5, "structure": "ONE", "targets": {"fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Did your first sentence already sound like that room?", "Did you use any word that room would not use?", "Was your length right for the room (shortest at the venue and with students)?", "Did you stop once the point was made?"]}, {"id": "words-4-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Same News, Two Registers", "how": "Deliver the news to the first room, pause three seconds, then deliver it to the second. The second version must not reuse a single sentence from the first.", "items": ["The music camp is cancelled. First to the council, then to the Year 9 band.", "A student has been suspended from the senior ensemble. First to staff, then to the student's parents.", "The festival lost money this year. First to the AJEA committee, then to the festival audience on the final night.", "A long-serving instrumental teacher is retiring. First to the staff meeting, then at the end-of-year ceremony.", "The band won its section at the competition. First to the band in the bus, then to the principal.", "The concert hall booking fell through. First to parents by announcement, then to the students in rehearsal."], "seconds": 60, "prep": 10, "structure": "WSN", "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did the two versions share facts but no sentences?", "Did your pace and sentence length change between them?", "Did you avoid talking down in the less formal version?", "Did you sound like the same person in both?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Deliver the passage in the register it was written for. Then deliver the same content as if from a jazz venue mic between tunes, in under half the time.", "seconds": 60, "checks": ["Did the first version honour its occasion?", "Was the venue version under half the length and still complete?", "Did every big word in either version earn its place?", "Did you keep your own voice across both?"]}}, {"id": "words-5", "title": "Phrases for Hard Moments", "goal": "Deliver a prepared, exact phrase for disagreeing, refusing, conceding, taking a question on notice, closing an item and interrupting, so the words arrive when adrenaline does.", "teach": {"body": "Under stress, word retrieval narrows. The adrenaline that sharpens your ears in a hard meeting also makes the right sentence harder to find, which is when the hedges and fillers rush in. Linguists have long observed that fluent speakers lean heavily on prefabricated phrases: chunks stored whole and retrieved whole. You know the principle from the instrument. A phrase you have practised until it sits under the fingers comes out at tempo when you have no time to think. These phrases are licks for the hard moments.\n\nDisagreeing. Name the shared aim, then the difference, then the reason. 'We both want the festival to break even. I see it differently: cutting the youth stage costs us more than it saves.' Avoid 'with respect', which signals the opposite, and 'no offence', which announces some.\n\nRefusing. One clear no, one reason, and an offer where you have one. 'I can't release the band on Thursday. I can on Monday.' One reason is stronger than three; every extra reason is one more thing to argue with. After the refusal, stop. The silence is part of the phrase.\n\nConceding. Specific and clean: 'You're right about the timing.' Then carry on. A concession made without wriggling raises your credibility on every point you do not concede. Do not bury it in 'I suppose that's partly fair'.\n\nTaking a question on notice. A parliamentary and committee term, and a useful one: you do not have the answer and will not invent it. 'I'll take that on notice and come back to you by Friday.' The date is not optional. Without it, 'on notice' sounds like a dodge.\n\nClosing an item. As chair, you summarise, state the decision, and name the next item: 'We'll leave it there. The fee stays at two hundred dollars, and Peter will report back in March. Next item.' The words 'next item' do the closing. Say them and turn the page.\n\nInterrupting gracefully. Use the person's name, raise a hand slightly, and give a reason that serves the room: 'Sarah, let me stop you there, because we're at time.' Or bridge: 'Can I pull us back to the question?' Name first; the name buys you the floor.\n\nDeliver all of these slightly slower and lower than the surrounding talk. Hard phrases rushed sound defensive. Said at an even pace, with a full stop and a beat of silence after, they sound like procedure, which is exactly what they are.", "model": "Thanks, Peter. || I see it *differently*. || We both want the festival to break even. | Cutting the youth stage | costs us more in ticket sales | than it saves in *fees*. || Let's take the figures *on notice*, | circulate them by *Friday*, | and decide at the November meeting. || Next *item*.", "mistake": "Yeah, look, I sort of hear what you're saying, Peter, and no offence, but I'm not sure that's maybe quite right, so, um, maybe we can sort of come back to it at some point?", "remember": ["Name the move, then make it.", "Refuse with one reason and an offer.", "Concede specifically, then keep going.", "Every 'on notice' gets a date.", "Close with the decision, then 'next item'."]}, "drills": [{"id": "words-5-d1", "type": "read", "title": "The Phrase Bank", "how": "Read each phrase slowly and slightly lower than your usual pitch, landing the stressed word and holding the full stop. Say each until it feels like your own.", "items": ["I see it *differently*. || Here's why.", "I can't do *Thursday*. | I can do *Monday*.", "You're *right* about that. || Let's go on.", "I'll take that *on notice* | and come back to you by *Friday*.", "We'll leave it there. | The decision is to *wait*. || Next *item*.", "Sarah, | let me stop you *there*, | because we're at time.", "Can I pull us back to the *question*?", "That's not something I can *agree* to."], "targets": {"fillers": 0, "wpm": [110, 140]}, "checks": ["Did you speak each phrase slower than normal conversation?", "Did each phrase end with a falling pitch and a held stop?", "Did any phrase pick up a hedge you didn't read?", "Did the meter stay inside the pace range?"]}, {"id": "words-5-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Wobble to Procedure", "how": "Say the wobbly version, then the procedural one. Notice how much shorter and calmer the second sounds.", "items": ["I'm not sure I totally agree, no offence. => I see it *differently*. || Here's why.", "I'd love to but it's really hard because of, um, lots of things. => I can't. || The band has a *competition* that week.", "I suppose that's sort of partly fair. => You're *right* about the cost.", "I'm not sure, I'll try to find out at some stage. => I'll take that on notice | and email you by *Wednesday*.", "So, um, yeah, should we maybe move on? => The decision is to *defer*. || Next item.", "Sorry, sorry, can I just jump in? => Michael, | let me *stop* you there."], "checks": ["Did each strong line name its move clearly?", "Did the on-notice line include a specific day?", "Did the interruption begin with a name?", "Did you stop after each strong line?"]}, {"id": "words-5-d3", "type": "hold", "title": "Hold After the No", "how": "Say each hard line as if to a real person in the room, then hold silence for the full count. Do not soften, explain or add a second reason in the silence.", "items": ["No. | The band won't be available that night.", "I don't agree, | and I won't be voting for it.", "That decision is *final* for this year.", "You're right. | I got that *wrong*.", "I'll take that on notice | and answer you by *Friday*.", "We're out of time. || Next *item*."], "hold": 4, "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did you hold the full four seconds after every line?", "Did you resist adding a softener in the silence?", "Did you keep eye level steady during the hold?", "Did the silence feel like part of the sentence by the end?"]}, {"id": "words-5-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Under Fire", "how": "Respond to each moment as it happens, using the right phrase from the bank within your first sentence. Acknowledge, bridge to your position, answer, then stop.", "items": ["A council member says the music program is a luxury the school can't afford.", "A parent insists their child should skip straight to the senior band.", "A colleague says your rehearsal schedule is wrecking their classes.", "A committee member keeps talking past time on an item you need to close.", "A journalist asks why the festival lost money when ticket sales were up.", "The principal asks you to cut a concert from the calendar."], "seconds": 45, "prep": 8, "structure": "ABA", "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did you name the move (disagree, refuse, concede, on notice) early?", "Did you acknowledge without apologising?", "Did you give one reason rather than a list?", "Did you end on a statement and stop?"]}], "apply": {"source": "scenario", "task": "Answer the scenario aloud as if the person is in front of you. Use at least one phrase from the bank word for word, and end with a clear decision or next step.", "seconds": 60, "structure": "ABA", "checks": ["Did you use a bank phrase without hedging it?", "Did you end with a decision, offer or date?", "Did you hold silence after your final sentence?", "Did you stay slower and lower than your usual pace?"]}}, {"id": "words-6", "title": "Devices Worth Owning", "goal": "Name and deploy six rhetorical devices on purpose (tricolon, antithesis, anaphora, the rule of three, the callback and the short sentence after the long one) at the right dose for each room.", "teach": {"body": "Rhetorical devices are forms, in the way AABA or a twelve-bar is a form. They work because the ear predicts a pattern and enjoys it completing. Used on purpose they make a line memorable. Stacked, they sound like a politician. Here are six worth owning, named so you can choose them.\n\nThe rule of three is the grouping principle: lists of three feel complete, two feels unfinished, four feels like a list. 'Before school, after school, and on weekends.' The tricolon is its tightest form: three parallel units of the same grammatical shape, ideally rising in length, with the longest last. Caesar's 'I came, I saw, I conquered' is the compact version; Lincoln's 'of the people, by the people, for the people' is the rhythmic one. Rising length matters because English stress falls late, so the longest unit carries the weight.\n\nAntithesis sets two opposites in parallel so each sharpens the other. Kennedy's 'not because they are easy, but because they are hard' is the model. Keep the grammar of both halves matched, and stress the contrasting words.\n\nAnaphora repeats the same opening across successive phrases. Churchill's 'we shall fight' is the famous case. It builds intensity and gives the listener a handle. Three repetitions is usually the right number; in a meeting, two.\n\nThe callback returns to an image, phrase or joke from earlier. Comedians use the term; musicians know it as the head coming back after the solos. It tells the room the speech had a shape and it is now closing. Plant the line early and plainly, then return to it in the last thirty seconds, preferably with its meaning changed.\n\nThe short sentence after the long one is the simplest device and the most useful. A long sentence builds tension: clauses accumulating, the listener holding the thread. Then a short one. It lands like a downbeat. In speech the short sentence takes natural stress, so you barely need to push it.\n\nDose by room. In a staff or council meeting, one device per item is plenty: a single antithesis or a clean short sentence. At a concert opening or close, two or three. At a ceremony or a toast you can use all of them, provided each is doing a job. If you notice the device before the meaning, it is too much.\n\nThe method: draft the plain version first. Then find the one sentence that matters most and give it a form.", "model": "In February | this band couldn't play a *B-flat* scale together. || Since then they have rehearsed before school, | after school, | and on a bus that broke down outside *Bunbury*. || They didn't get here because it was *easy*. | They got here because they kept *turning up*. || Remember that B-flat scale? || *Listen*.", "mistake": "Thanks everyone, the band has worked really hard this year and improved a lot, so yeah, enjoy the concert.", "remember": ["Draft plain first, then give one sentence a form.", "Make the third item the longest.", "Match the grammar on both sides of a contrast.", "Plant early what you plan to call back.", "After a long sentence, land a short one."]}, "drills": [{"id": "words-6-d1", "type": "read", "title": "Name It, Then Say It", "how": "Before each item, say the device's name aloud. Then read the line, stressing the starred words so the form is audible.", "items": ["Tricolon: | We rehearse *early*, | we rehearse *often*, | and we rehearse until it *sounds like music*.", "Antithesis: | The band doesn't need more *talent*. | It needs more *time*.", "Anaphora: | *Every* student auditioned. | *Every* student was heard. | *Every* student has a place.", "Short after long: | We spent the year chasing halls, fixing stands, rebuilding the timetable and replacing a bass that had been cracked since before most of you were born. || It was *worth* it.", "Rule of three: | Tonight you'll hear a *ballad*, | a *blues*, | and something by *Monk*.", "Antithesis: | We don't teach music to make *musicians*. | We teach music to make *listeners*."], "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did you say the device name before each line?", "Was the form audible from the stress alone?", "In the tricolon, did the last unit feel heaviest?", "Did the short sentence land with less effort than the long one?"]}, {"id": "words-6-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Give It a Form", "how": "Say the flat line, then the line with a device. Name the device silently to yourself before the strong line.", "items": ["The festival matters for students and for the community. => The festival is where students *perform*, | families *listen*, | and a city remembers it has a jazz *scene*.", "Practice is more important than ability. => Ability gets you in the *door*. | Practice keeps you in the *room*.", "We need the room, the funding and the staff. => We need the *room*. | We need the *funding*. | And we need the people to *teach* in it.", "It was a long and difficult term but we got there. => It was a term of cancelled rehearsals, two floods and one very patient bus driver. || We *made* it.", "This award recognises hard work. => This award isn't for the most *gifted* player. | It's for the one who never *stopped*.", "Music teaches discipline, teamwork and confidence. => Music teaches you to *count*, | to *listen*, | and to stand up in front of four hundred people and *mean* it."], "checks": ["Could you name the device in each strong line?", "In each contrast, did both halves share the same grammar?", "Did every three-part line rise in length?", "Did the strong line sound like you rather than a politician?"]}, {"id": "words-6-d3", "type": "repeat", "title": "Tune the Tricolon", "how": "Say each tricolon three times. First at an even pace. Second with a small pause before the third unit. Third slowing slightly through the third unit and dropping pitch on its last word.", "items": ["They came *early*, | they stayed *late*, | and they played like it *mattered*.", "Read the *chart*, | watch the *stick*, | and listen to the person next to *you*.", "For the *parents*, | for the *staff*, | and for every student who carried a bass up those *stairs*.", "We plan it, | we rehearse it, | and then we let it *go*.", "Thank you for your *time*, | your *patience*, | and your *ears*."], "checks": ["Could you hear three different shapes across the passes?", "On the third pass, did the final word fall in pitch?", "Did the pause before the third unit make it land harder?", "Did any pass tip into sounding theatrical?"]}, {"id": "words-6-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Build It Live", "how": "Speak on each occasion using at least two named devices and a callback from your first sentence to your last. Plain words; let the forms do the lifting.", "items": ["A thirty-second toast at a colleague's farewell.", "Closing the end-of-year concert.", "Opening the festival's final night from the stage.", "Presenting the music prize at the award ceremony.", "The last word to the senior band before their final performance.", "Opening the first school council meeting of the year."], "seconds": 60, "prep": 15, "structure": "PPF", "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did your last sentence call back to your first?", "Could you name at least two devices you used?", "Did you follow at least one long sentence with a short one?", "Was the dose right for that occasion?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Deliver the passage as written. Then name aloud each device you can find in it, and deliver it again with one added callback and one short sentence after a long one.", "seconds": 90, "checks": ["Did you find and name every device in the passage?", "Did your added callback return to something from the opening?", "Did your short sentence land on the downbeat?", "Did the second delivery feel stronger without feeling heavier?"]}}]}, {"strand": "Body", "intro": "This strand trains what the room sees before it hears a word: stance, hands, eyes, face, position and the body under adrenaline. You already carry physical authority behind a bass and in front of a band; the work here is keeping it in rooms where you have no instrument and no baton, and holding it when the stakes rise.", "units": [{"id": "body-1", "title": "Stance and stillness", "goal": "Plant a stance before the first word and hold it through the opening without sway, shift or locked knees.", "teach": {"body": "Stillness is not the absence of movement. It is the base every other movement starts from and returns to, and a room reads it as permission to settle. An audience tracks motion before it tracks meaning: peripheral vision is built to notice change, so a sway, a weight shift or a heel bounce pulls attention off the words and onto the body. You know this from the bandstand. A drummer who rushes moves the whole band; a speaker who sways moves the whole room, and the room rarely knows why it feels uneasy.\n\nThe stance. Feet roughly under the hips, toes forward or very slightly out, weight spread across the whole foot rather than parked on the heels. Knees soft: not bent, just unlocked. Locked knees push the pelvis forward, stiffen the lower back and make long stretches of standing harder on circulation, which is why soldiers on parade are told never to lock them. Let the pelvis sit level, the sternum lift a fraction, the crown of the head rise. This is the bassist's stance without the instrument: grounded, upright, ready to move but not moving.\n\nWhat goes wrong. Under pressure the body looks for an exit, and it usually takes one of four: the side-to-side sway, the rock from heel to toe, the hip cocked onto one leg, or the slow drift backwards. The hip cock is the staff-meeting habit; it reads as casual, which suits a joke and undercuts a decision. The backward drift reads as retreat.\n\nThe planted start. Walk to your spot, stop, settle the weight, take one breath, find one person, then speak. Those three seconds feel enormous from the inside and look like composure from the outside. Never talk while still arriving: a first sentence spoken on the move is lost. On a concert stage this means you reach the microphone, plant, and let the applause or chatter finish before \"Good evening\". At a council table it means both feet flat on the floor before you open the meeting.\n\nHold the stillness through the first thirty seconds especially. That is when adrenaline peaks and the room is forming its judgement. After that you may move, and a later unit deals with how. But every movement should end back in this stance, the way a phrase resolves to the tonic.", "model": "[Walk on. Stop. Plant. One breath.] Good *evening*. | Welcome to the *Spring* Concert. || Tonight you'll hear every ensemble in the department, | from the Year Sevens who started in *February* | to the Big Band, | who started rather *earlier*. || Phones on silent, please. | Let's *begin*.", "mistake": "He starts \"Good evening\" while still walking to the mic, arrives mid-sentence, then rocks heel to toe through the welcome and has settled onto one hip by \"phones on silent\".", "remember": ["Arrive, plant, breathe, then speak.", "Unlock the knees; keep them soft.", "Spread the weight across the whole foot.", "Never speak while still arriving.", "Every move resolves back to the stance."]}, "drills": [{"id": "body-1-d1", "type": "mirror", "title": "Find the stance", "how": "Prop the phone at chest height and step back until you can see from head to hips. Take each position, hold it, and say the line aloud while holding.", "items": ["Feet under the hips, toes forward. Say: 'Good evening, and welcome.'", "Lock your knees hard, then release them to soft. Say the welcome again and notice the change in your chest.", "Rock onto your heels, then onto your toes, then settle in the middle. Say: 'Let's begin.'", "Cock onto one hip, then return to centre. Say: 'This is a decision, not a discussion.'", "Lift the crown of the head a centimetre without lifting the chin. Say: 'Item one.'", "Weight even, nothing moving for the whole hold. Say: 'Thank you for coming tonight.' Then stay still."], "seconds": 20, "checks": ["Did your head stay in the same place in the frame?", "Were both knees unlocked throughout?", "Did your hips stay level, with no cocked side?", "Did you speak only once you were settled?"]}, {"id": "body-1-d2", "type": "mirror", "title": "The planted start", "how": "Start two steps out of frame. Walk in, stop, plant, take one breath, look at the lens, then say the line, and hold the stance to the end of the time.", "items": ["Good evening, and welcome to the Winter Concert.", "Welcome, everyone. I'll open the meeting.", "Good evening. This is the Year Seven parent information evening.", "Thanks, everyone. Two things before we play.", "Good evening. I look after the music department here.", "Order, please. We have a quorum."], "seconds": 15, "checks": ["Was there a clear stop before the first word?", "Did the first word come after the breath, not during the walk?", "Had your head stopped moving before you spoke?", "Did you stay planted through the last word?"]}, {"id": "body-1-d3", "type": "hold", "title": "Stillness through silence", "how": "Stand in your stance. Say each line, then hold the silence without moving anything below the neck.", "items": ["Before we go on, | I want to say *one* thing.", "The motion is *carried*.", "That was the best they've *ever* played it.", "We are *not* cancelling the tour.", "I'll take *questions* now.", "Thank you. || Good *night*."], "hold": 4, "checks": ["Did your body stay still during the silence?", "Did you resist shifting your weight as the silence ended?", "Did your hands stay where they were?", "Did the silence feel chosen rather than stuck?"]}, {"id": "body-1-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Planted for a minute", "how": "Walk to your spot and plant before the timer starts. Speak on the topic without moving your feet once.", "items": ["Why every student should play in an ensemble, not just take lessons.", "Should a concert start on time if half the audience is still parking?", "The council should fund a second set of timpani.", "Why the Big Band rehearses before school.", "What a parent should listen for at their child's first concert.", "Why the program holds auditions at all."], "seconds": 60, "prep": 15, "structure": "PREP", "targets": {"fillers": 3}, "checks": ["Did your feet stay planted for the whole minute?", "Did you plant before the first word?", "Did you finish in the stance rather than turning away?", "Did you avoid the hip cock in the middle?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Deliver the passage standing, with the phone propped as a mirror. Walk in, plant, breathe and find a point before the first word, and do not move your feet until the last word has landed.", "seconds": 90, "checks": ["Did you plant before speaking?", "Did your feet stay still throughout?", "Were your knees as soft at the end as at the start?", "Did you hold still for a beat after the final word?"]}}, {"id": "body-2", "title": "Hands with a purpose", "goal": "Rest the hands in a neutral home position and make gestures that land with the words, including with a microphone, notes or a baton in hand.", "teach": {"body": "Hands are the most watched part of a speaker below the face, and the part most likely to leak nerves. The aim is not fewer gestures. It is gestures that mean something, and a rest position to return to between them.\n\nThe home position. Arms hang loosely at your sides, or the hands meet lightly in front at about navel height, fingertips touching or one hand resting in the other. It will feel slightly too open at first. What it replaces is worse: arms folded (a wall), hands behind the back (hiding), hands in pockets (fiddling with keys within a minute), or the fig leaf, hands clasped low in front, which shrinks you. A tight steeple or a wringing grip is fidgeting in disguise.\n\nGestures that match words. The psycholinguist David McNeill classified the gestures people make spontaneously, and his categories are useful. Deictic gestures point: the brass, the back row, the agenda. Iconic gestures draw the thing: the size of the hall, the arc of a crescendo. Metaphoric gestures give shape to an idea: two hands weighing two options. Beat gestures are small downward strokes that mark stress, like a conductor's ictus. Gesture research is consistent on one point: gesturing helps the speaker find words, not only the listener follow them. Sitting on your hands makes you less fluent.\n\nTiming is the craft. A gesture should land on the stressed word or just before it, never after; a late gesture looks like an afterthought. Keep most gestures in a box from shoulders to waist, a little wider than your body. Above the shoulders reads as alarm; below the waist disappears. Make the gesture, hold it through the word, then return home. The return is what makes the next gesture count.\n\nObjects. A microphone sits about a fist below the chin, angled at the mouth, and stays there; the other hand gestures. When you turn your head, the mic turns with it. Notes are held in one hand at waist height or left on the lectern, and never rolled, folded or tapped. The baton is the hardest, because you have spent years making it mean time. When you talk to the band, put it down or point the tip at the floor. A baton moving during speech still reads as beat to the players.\n\nFidgeting is self-touch and repetition: pen clicks, sleeve tugs, a hand to the face, a ring turned. The cure is not willpower. It is a home position you trust, and gestures that serve the words.", "model": "There are *two* problems with the Saturday rehearsal. [Two fingers, then home.] | The *first* is the hall. [Left hand.] || The *second* is the bus. [Right hand.] || We can fix the *hall*. | We *can't* fix the bus. || So we move to *Sunday*. [Both hands open, then home.]", "mistake": "He says \"there are two problems\" with both hands clasped at his belt, lifts the left hand on \"the bus\" a beat after the word has gone, clicks his pen through \"we can't fix\", and waves the pen on \"Sunday\".", "remember": ["Return home between gestures.", "Land the gesture on the stressed word.", "Keep gestures between shoulders and waist.", "Mic still to the mouth; the other hand talks.", "Put the baton down to speak."]}, "drills": [{"id": "body-2-d1", "type": "mirror", "title": "Find home", "how": "Frame yourself from head to waist. Take each position, hold it, and say the line while in it; notice which versions look like hiding.", "items": ["Arms loose at your sides. Say: 'Good evening, everyone.'", "Hands meeting lightly at navel height. Say the same line.", "Arms folded. Say the line, then drop them to home and say it again.", "Hands in the fig leaf, low in front. Say the line, then lift them to navel height and repeat.", "Hands behind your back. Say: 'I'm happy to take questions.' Then bring them forward and repeat.", "Home position, no movement for the whole hold. Say: 'Item three is the budget.'"], "seconds": 15, "checks": ["Did your hands stay still in home without fiddling?", "Did your shoulders drop once your arms were open?", "Were both hands visible in the frame throughout?", "Did the open versions look more assured than the closed ones?"]}, {"id": "body-2-d2", "type": "mirror", "title": "Gesture on the word", "how": "Say each line with one gesture that lands exactly on the stressed word, hold it through the word, then return home. Watch the timing in the mirror.", "items": ["Deictic, point to one side: 'The *brass* come in at bar forty.'", "Iconic, show the size: 'The hall holds *six hundred* people.'", "Metaphoric, weigh two options in two hands: 'We can tour, | or we can *record*.'", "Beat, small downward strokes: 'This | is | *not* | negotiable.'", "Two fingers: 'I have *two* questions.'", "Open palms, outward: 'The floor is *yours*.'"], "seconds": 12, "checks": ["Did the gesture land on the stressed word, not after it?", "Did you return home before the next line?", "Did the gesture stay inside the shoulder-to-waist box?", "Did the gesture carry the meaning rather than just add movement?"]}, {"id": "body-2-d3", "type": "mirror", "title": "Microphone, notes, baton", "how": "Use the real object or a stand-in: a bottle for the mic, a card for notes, a pencil for the baton. Follow each instruction while saying the line, and watch the object as closely as the face.", "items": ["Mic a fist below the chin, angled at the mouth. Turn your head left and right on: 'Good evening, and thanks for being here.' Keep the mic with the mouth.", "Mic in one hand, the other gestures once on the stressed word: 'Our next piece is by Duke *Ellington*.'", "Notes in one hand at waist height. Glance down, look up, then say: 'Three reports tonight.'", "Notes on the table, hands home. Say: 'I'll read the motion exactly as written.' Touch nothing but the page edge.", "Baton in hand, tip lowered to the floor before you speak: 'Bar fifty-two, from the pickup.'", "Baton down on the stand: 'That was better. | *Much* better.' Then pick it up for the downbeat."], "seconds": 20, "checks": ["Did the mic stay the same distance from your mouth?", "Did the notes stay still, never rolled or tapped?", "Was the baton still while you spoke?", "Did the free hand gesture on the stressed word?"]}, {"id": "body-2-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Explain it with hands", "how": "Stand with hands in home and explain the topic. Use at least one pointing, one shaping and one weighing gesture, returning home between them.", "items": ["How a big band is laid out on stage.", "Concert pitch and written pitch, explained for parents.", "Why the budget has two separate lines for instrument repairs.", "How the Year Seven auditions run, start to finish.", "The two options for next year's tour.", "Where parents park and where students enter on concert night."], "seconds": 60, "prep": 15, "structure": "TWO", "checks": ["Did you start and finish in home position?", "Did you use a pointing, a shaping and a weighing gesture?", "Did you get through the minute without touching your face or clothes?", "Did each gesture arrive with its word, not after it?"]}], "apply": {"source": "direction", "task": "Give the direction to the mirror three ways: hands in home throughout, then with one gesture landing on its key word, then holding a mic or baton stand-in. Keep the version where the hand and the word arrive together.", "seconds": 20, "checks": ["Did the gesture land on the key word?", "Did you return home after it?", "Did the object stay still while you spoke?", "Did the direction sound firmer with the gesture than without?"]}}, {"id": "body-3", "title": "Eyes, one per thought", "goal": "Hold one person's eyes for one complete thought, move deliberately through a hall or around a table, and look away to think without losing the room.", "teach": {"body": "Eye contact is the main channel by which a room decides whether you are talking to it or at it. The usual advice, scan the room, produces the lighthouse: a slow sweep that touches everyone and connects with no one. The better rule is one person per thought.\n\nOne person per thought. Choose one person and give them a complete thought, a sentence or a clause that ends in a pause, then move to someone else for the next. In a large room each look lasts a few seconds; at close quarters, a little less. The person you look at feels addressed, and so do the people around them, because from a distance a look at one person reads as a look at their section. Moving your eyes mid-sentence breaks the thought in half. Moving at the pause binds eye and phrase together, and slows you down.\n\nScanning a hall. Divide the room into zones: front left, front right, back left, back right, centre, balcony if there is one. Visit them out of order so it doesn't become a pattern, and give the back corners their share. Under stage lights you may not see faces past the third row. Look where the faces would be, at eye height, and the effect is the same. Avoid the clock, the ceiling and the exit sign.\n\nThe council table. Close quarters change the rules. Hold the eyes of the person you are answering. When you address the whole table, move person to person at each thought, including the people beside you, who are the easiest to forget. When someone else is speaking, look at them, steadily. As chair, your eyes tell the table who has the floor: when you turn your head to the next speaker, you have given it to them.\n\nLooking away to think. Research on gaze aversion is consistent: people look away when retrieving or constructing something difficult, because faces take a lot of processing and looking away frees capacity. So it is legitimate, and you should do it on purpose. Look down or to the side, not up at the ceiling, which reads as searching for an answer that isn't there. Keep the body and face toward the room, and bring your eyes back to a person before the answer starts. Think away, speak to someone.\n\nWhat goes wrong. Reading notes with occasional glances up, which gives the room your forehead. Talking to the one friendly face for the whole speech. Watching the principal while addressing the staff. Looking back at the slide. And the one to watch in yourself: eyes darting as fast as ideas arrive, so the room sees a tangent coming before it hears one.", "model": "[To one parent, front left.] When the Year Sevens started in February, | *half* of them couldn't read a note. || [Back right.] Tonight they're playing in *three* parts. || [Centre.] That is the work of their teachers, | and *their* work, | [front right] and *yours*, | getting them here at seven forty-five every *Wednesday*.", "mistake": "He reads \"when the Year Sevens started\" off the page, glances at the ceiling for the next phrase, then sweeps the hall on \"tonight they're playing in three parts\" without landing on anyone.", "remember": ["One person, one thought, then move.", "Move your eyes at the pause.", "Give the back corners their share.", "Think away, speak to someone.", "Look at whoever has the floor."]}, "drills": [{"id": "body-3-d1", "type": "mirror", "title": "Hold for the thought", "how": "Treat the lens as one person's eyes. Say each line without your eyes leaving the lens until the final word has landed, then look away for one second.", "items": ["The concert starts at *seven*.", "I'd like to hear from *you* on this one.", "We have *two* options, | and I prefer the *second*.", "Your son is doing *well*.", "The motion is *carried*.", "That entry was *late*, | and I think you know *why*."], "seconds": 10, "checks": ["Did your eyes stay on the lens until the last word?", "Did you blink normally rather than stare?", "Did the look away come after the line, not during it?", "Did your face stay warm while you held the look?"]}, {"id": "body-3-d2", "type": "read", "title": "Zones of the hall", "how": "Stand facing a room and assign five zones to five objects, including two far corners. Read each item aloud, moving to a new zone at every | or ||, never mid-phrase.", "items": ["Good evening. | Welcome to the *Spring* Concert. || We have *five* ensembles tonight, | and an interval after the *third*.", "Thank you all for coming. | I know it's a *school* night. || We'll be finished by *nine*.", "The Junior Band started in *February*. || Some of them had *never* held an instrument. || Listen for the *clarinets*.", "There's tea in the foyer at *interval*. || Please be back in your seats by the *bell*.", "Before we go, | *one* thank you. || To the parents who *drove*, | every *week*."], "checks": ["Did every eye move happen at a pause mark?", "Did all five zones get at least one thought?", "Did the far corners get a look?", "Did you avoid a left-to-right pattern?"]}, {"id": "body-3-d3", "type": "mirror", "title": "Look away, come back", "how": "Read the question aloud, look down or to the side to think, then bring your eyes back to the lens before the first word of your answer. Answer in one or two sentences.", "items": ["A parent: why didn't my daughter get into the top concert band?", "A councillor: what would you cut if the music budget fell by a fifth?", "The principal: is the tour worth the risk?", "A journalist: why does a public school need a specialist music program?", "A student: why do we have to play scales?", "A colleague: what's the hardest part of running the department?"], "seconds": 20, "checks": ["Did you look down or sideways, not up?", "Did your eyes return before the first word?", "Did your face and body stay toward the lens while you looked away?", "Was the look away one deliberate move, not darting?"]}, {"id": "body-3-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Around the table", "how": "Sit at a table and place four objects around it as members, two of them beside you. Speak on the item, giving each thought to a different member and finishing on one of them.", "items": ["Report to the council on concert attendance this year.", "Explain the new rehearsal timetable to the department.", "Brief the leadership team on the state of the tour.", "Tell the council what the parent survey said about concert times.", "Update staff on the feeder primary band program.", "Explain to the department why the instrument audit is overdue."], "seconds": 60, "prep": 20, "structure": "WSN", "checks": ["Did each thought go to a single member?", "Did the two beside you get a look?", "Did you look away only to think, and come back to speak?", "Did you finish on one person, not on the tabletop?"]}], "apply": {"source": "scenario", "task": "Answer the scenario aloud to an imagined table of four. Look away to think before you begin, then give each thought of your answer to a different person, finishing on the one who asked.", "seconds": 60, "structure": "ABA", "checks": ["Did you look away to think, then return before speaking?", "Did each thought go to one person?", "Did you finish on the person who asked?", "Did your eyes stay still within each thought?"]}}, {"id": "body-4", "title": "Face and resting expression", "goal": "Keep a resting face that reads as warm and attentive, listen visibly without nodding along, and take criticism with a face that stays open.", "teach": {"body": "Your face broadcasts when you are not speaking. In a meeting you chair you spend far more time listening than talking, and the room reads your face the whole time. Many people, autistic people often among them, have a resting expression that others read as stern, bored or displeased when they are simply concentrating. You cannot argue the room out of that reading, but you can set a different default.\n\nThe resting face. Not a smile. A smile held for minutes turns into a grin, and a grin under pressure reads as nerves or contempt. The resting face is a fractional lift: the corners of the mouth released rather than pulled, the jaw unclenched with the teeth slightly apart, the forehead smooth, the eyes soft. Thinking of someone you like does most of it for you. This is warmth without performance, and it is the face you want between agenda items and on the downbeat of an announcement.\n\nThe real smile. The nineteenth-century neurologist Guillaume Duchenne described the difference between a social smile, made with the mouth, and a felt smile, which also narrows the eyes through the muscle that rings them. People tend to read the difference without knowing they are doing it. You cannot fake the second for long, so save the smile for moments that earn it, and let it arrive and leave. A smile that fades naturally is credible; one that switches off is not.\n\nThe listening face. Look at the speaker, keep the resting face, and let the eyebrows do the work: a slight lift when something is new, a slight draw together when it is complicated. Nod occasionally and slowly, on their main points. Fast, constant nodding reads as impatience, as if you are hurrying them to finish, and from the chair it can look like agreement you have not given. A slow nod says \"I've heard that\", not \"I agree\".\n\nThe criticised face. When a parent or councillor criticises you or the program, the face goes to one of three places: the tight mouth, the raised eyebrows of disbelief, or the half-smile, which is the worst because it reads as dismissal. Instead, hold the resting face, keep looking at them, and let one slow nod acknowledge the point. Breathe out through the nose. Do not draft your reply on your face; draft it in the pause after they finish. A face that stays open under criticism tells the room you are not threatened, which persuades before you say a word.\n\nFinally, the face has to match the words. Bad news with a smile, thanks with a frown: the room believes the face.", "model": "Thank you, | that's a *fair* question. || You're right that the concert ran *late*. || It ran late because we added the *Year Sevens*, | and I'd do that *again*. || What I'll change | is the *interval*.", "mistake": "He listens to the complaint with a half-smile and quick nods, then answers through a tight mouth, so \"that's a fair question\" means the opposite.", "remember": ["Release the mouth; unclench the jaw.", "Save the full smile for moments that earn it.", "Nod slowly, on main points only.", "Don't draft your reply on your face.", "Make the face match the words."]}, "drills": [{"id": "body-4-d1", "type": "mirror", "title": "Set the resting face", "how": "Frame your face closely. Do each instruction while watching, then say the line holding the result.", "items": ["Clench the jaw, then release until the teeth part slightly. Say: 'Let's move to item four.'", "Frown hard, then let the forehead go smooth. Say the same line.", "Think of someone you like, without smiling. Say: 'Good evening, everyone.'", "Grin broadly for five seconds, then drop to the resting face. Say: 'Welcome back after the interval.'", "Resting face, eyes on the lens. Say: 'Go on, I'm listening.'", "Resting face, then let a real smile arrive and leave on: 'And that was the Year Sevens.'"], "seconds": 15, "checks": ["Were your teeth apart and your jaw loose?", "Was your forehead smooth?", "Did the resting face look warm rather than blank?", "Did the smile fade gradually rather than switch off?"]}, {"id": "body-4-d2", "type": "mirror", "title": "The listening face", "how": "Say the other person's line aloud, then look at the lens and hold the listening face for the rest of the time before giving your one-line reply.", "items": ["'I think the concert was far too long this year.' Then: 'Thank you. I've heard that.'", "'The council hasn't seen a budget breakdown in two years.' Then: 'That's fair.'", "'My son says rehearsals are a waste of his time.' Then: 'Tell me more about that.'", "'I'd like to propose we move the meeting to Thursdays.' Then: 'Noted. Anyone else?'", "'The Year Nines were very loud in the corridor.' Then: 'I'll speak to them.'", "'That arrangement is the best thing the band has done.' Then: 'Thank you. That means a lot.'"], "seconds": 20, "checks": ["Did you nod slowly and no more than twice?", "Did your eyebrows move rather than your mouth?", "Did you keep your reply off your face while listening?", "Did the reply match the face you gave it?"]}, {"id": "body-4-d3", "type": "hold", "title": "Take the hit", "how": "Say each criticism aloud as if someone has just said it to you, hold the silence with the resting face and one slow nod, then say only 'Thank you.'", "items": ["You've favoured the Big Band over every other *ensemble*.", "Frankly, | the concert was *disorganised*.", "Other schools manage to finish rehearsals on *time*.", "The council was *never* told about this.", "My daughter says you *embarrassed* her in front of the band.", "I'm not sure you're the right person to *lead* this."], "hold": 3, "checks": ["Did you avoid the half-smile?", "Did your eyebrows stay level rather than rise?", "Did you breathe out before 'thank you'?", "Did 'thank you' sound steady rather than clipped?"]}, {"id": "body-4-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Criticised, then answer", "how": "Read the criticism aloud, hold the open face for one breath, then answer. Keep the face matched to each part of the answer: serious where it is serious, warm where it is warm.", "items": ["A parent at a parent evening: 'Music takes up too much of my child's time.'", "A councillor: 'Why is music getting more money than sport?'", "A colleague in a staff meeting: 'Your concert clashed with our exams.'", "A student: 'The saxes never get a solo.'", "A panel host: 'Isn't specialist music only for kids whose parents can afford lessons?'", "The principal: 'I had three complaints about the concert finishing late.'"], "seconds": 45, "prep": 10, "structure": "ABA", "checks": ["Did your face stay open while the criticism landed?", "Did the acknowledgement sound and look meant?", "Did your face match each part of the answer?", "Did you avoid smiling through the difficult part?"]}], "apply": {"source": "pair", "task": "Say the weak phrasing, then the strong one, into the mirror. On the strong one, check that your face carries the same meaning as the words; where they disagree, the room believes the face.", "seconds": 20, "checks": ["Did your face match the strong line's meaning?", "Was your jaw loose throughout?", "Did any smile arrive only where the words earned it?", "Did the strong line look as different as it sounded?"]}}, {"id": "body-5", "title": "Space and movement", "goal": "Choose where to stand in any room, move only on transitions, and use a table or lectern without hiding behind it.", "teach": {"body": "Where you stand tells the room who you are in relation to it before you speak. Movement then either reinforces that or erodes it. The principle is simple: move on the transition, plant for the point.\n\nMoving with purpose. Every move should mean something: a new section, a change of subject, a step toward the audience for something personal, a step back to give the stage to the band. Walk during the link sentence (\"Which brings me to the tour.\"), then plant and deliver the next point still. The common fault is pacing, a steady walk with no destination that the audience follows like a tennis rally. The subtler one is the drift, small steps forward and back that you never notice and everyone else does. If you move, take at least two or three clear steps, and stop completely.\n\nThe stage with a band behind you. You are the frame, not the picture. Stand downstage, near the front edge, and to one side of centre, usually away from the soloists' microphones, so the audience can see the ensemble past your shoulder. Angle slightly toward the band when you introduce them and fully to the audience when you address it. Never talk with your back to the audience, and never talk over the band tuning. When you hand over, turn and take two steps toward the podium, and the move becomes the announcement. Between tunes at a jazz venue it is the same in miniature: step to the mic, plant, say it, step back into the band.\n\nStanding versus sitting. Standing gives you voice, height and command: use it to open a meeting with stakes, to make an announcement, to address a parent evening of more than a small group. Sitting signals collaboration: use it for discussion, for a hard one-to-one, for the principal's office. At a table, sit back in the chair with both feet on the floor, forearms on the table, hands visible. Leaning in slightly signals engagement on a point; leaning back for a long stretch reads as withdrawal or judgement. Standing up in a meeting where everyone else is seated changes the temperature, so do it only when you mean to.\n\nThe lectern. It is a shelf for notes, not a shield. Stand a hand's width behind it, upright, hands resting lightly on its edges or in home position, never gripping and never leaning. For a story or a moment of warmth, step to its side, which is closer and more human, and return to it for the formal parts: acknowledgements, lists, names read out. At an award ceremony, step back from the lectern while the recipient comes up, and applaud them; the space is theirs.", "model": "That was the *Junior* Band. || [Two steps toward the band, talking.] Which brings us | to the *Big* Band. || [Plant.] They've rehearsed at seven every *Tuesday* morning since *February*. || [Turn, one step back.] Ladies and gentlemen, | the *Big* Band.", "mistake": "He wanders across the front of the stage while introducing the Big Band, stops half-turned to look at the players, and announces the band's name to the drummer.", "remember": ["Move on the transition, plant for the point.", "Stand downstage, to one side of the band.", "Stand for stakes, sit for discussion.", "The lectern is a shelf, not a shield.", "When you move, stop completely."]}, "drills": [{"id": "body-5-d1", "type": "mirror", "title": "Move, then plant", "how": "Prop the phone at the end of a space with room for three steps. Say the link sentence while you walk, stop completely, then deliver the point standing still.", "items": ["Walk: 'Which brings me to the tour.' Plant: 'It's going *ahead*.'", "Walk: 'Now, the budget.' Plant: 'We're *under* by four hundred dollars.'", "Walk toward the lens: 'Something personal.' Plant: 'This is my *twentieth* year teaching.'", "Walk back: 'And so, to the music.' Plant: 'The *Junior* Band.'", "Walk: 'One more thing.' Plant: 'Please take your *rubbish* with you.'", "Stay still for both: 'Thank you.' || 'Good *night*.'"], "seconds": 15, "checks": ["Did you walk only during the link sentence?", "Did you stop fully before the point?", "Were your feet still for the whole point?", "Did you take at least two clear steps, not a shuffle?"]}, {"id": "body-5-d2", "type": "mirror", "title": "Lectern and table", "how": "Use a bench, a table or a stack of books as the lectern. Follow each instruction while saying the line.", "items": ["Stand a hand's width behind it, hands resting lightly on the edges: 'Good evening, and welcome to the awards.'", "Grip it hard, then release. Say the same line again.", "Step to its side: 'When I first heard this student play, she was eleven.'", "Step back behind it: 'The award for Outstanding Contribution goes to...'", "Sit at a table, feet flat, forearms on the table, hands visible: 'Let's start. Item one.'", "Lean in for one point, then sit back to listen: 'This is the one I care about.' Then: 'Over to you.'"], "seconds": 20, "checks": ["Were your hands resting rather than gripping?", "Did you stand upright rather than lean on it?", "Did stepping to the side change your voice?", "At the table, were both hands visible?"]}, {"id": "body-5-d3", "type": "recall", "title": "Block the introduction", "how": "Read the anchor points, then deliver the talk from your spot, moving only between points and planting for each one.", "items": ["Welcome and phones / The Junior Band's first year / Walk: the Big Band / Tonight's soloist / Turn and hand over", "Welcome back from interval / Walk: the second half / The strings and their piece / Turn and hand over to the conductor", "Open the parent evening / What the program is / Walk: the three-year commitment / What it costs / Questions", "Open the council meeting / Apologies / Stand: the music report / Sit: discussion / Close the item", "Between tunes: the last tune / The next one and its composer / Who you learned it from / Step back and count in", "Why this award exists / Step to the side: the student / Back to the lectern: the name / Step back and applaud"], "seconds": 60, "prep": 20, "checks": ["Did you move only between points?", "Did you plant for every point?", "Did the handover include a physical move?", "Did you keep the points in order?"]}, {"id": "body-5-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Stand or sit", "how": "Decide first whether the moment calls for standing or sitting, take that position, then speak. Be in position before the first word, not moving into it.", "items": ["Tell the department the concert date has moved.", "Tell a parent, one to one, that their child hasn't made the top band.", "Open a council meeting with a contentious vote on the agenda.", "Thank the staff at the end-of-year function.", "Ask the principal for more staffing in the department.", "Tell the band the tour is cancelled."], "seconds": 45, "prep": 15, "structure": "ONE", "checks": ["Did you choose a position before you spoke?", "Did the position suit the moment?", "Did you stay in it without drifting?", "If standing, were you planted for the main point?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Block the passage before you speak it: where you stand, the one place you move, and where you plant again. Deliver it with that blocking, moving only on the transition.", "seconds": 90, "checks": ["Did you move only once, on the transition?", "Did you plant fully before the next point?", "Did your position suit the room the passage is for?", "Did you finish still, facing the audience?"]}}, {"id": "body-6", "title": "The body under adrenaline", "goal": "Reduce the visible effects of adrenaline and use the minute before speaking to arrive steady, slow and planted.", "teach": {"body": "The body before a high-stakes moment is not malfunctioning. It is doing what it evolved to do: adrenaline is released, heart rate and breathing rise, blood moves toward the large muscles, digestion and saliva are turned down, and fine motor control loosens. That is why the hands shake, the mouth dries and the heart races. You know the version that arrives before a gig. The version before a difficult council meeting is the same chemistry with less rehearsal behind it.\n\nTwo findings are worth knowing. First, arousal and fear feel nearly identical in the body. Alison Wood Brooks at Harvard found that people who told themselves \"I am excited\" before speaking performed better than people who tried to calm down. Going from high arousal to calm is a big jump; relabelling the same arousal as readiness is a small one. Second, the room sees far less than you feel. Psychologists call this the illusion of transparency: we overestimate how visible our internal state is to others, and studies of public speakers show the effect directly.\n\nBreath is the most direct control you have. Heart rate rises slightly on each in-breath and falls on each out-breath, so a long exhale slows it. The working pattern is in through the nose for about four, a brief pause, and out through pursed lips for six or seven. A variant studied more recently is the physiological sigh: two inhales through the nose, the second topping up the first, then one long exhale through the mouth. Two or three of these, not twenty.\n\nShaking hands. Fine tremor shows most in hands that are unsupported and trying to stay still. Give them something with weight, rest them on a lectern edge, or let them hang. Before you go on, press the palms together hard for five seconds and release, or squeeze both fists; working the large muscles uses some of the readiness. Never hold a single sheet of paper, which amplifies every tremor. Use card.\n\nDry mouth. Sip room-temperature water in the minutes before, not iced and not a full glass. Press the tongue to the roof of the mouth or lightly bite its tip to start saliva.\n\nRacing heart and rushing. Adrenaline speeds your internal clock, so your sense of normal pace runs fast. Plan to start slower than feels right, with a first sentence you could say in your sleep.\n\nThe minute before. Stance. Three long exhales. Press the palms, release. Sip. Say the first line under your breath. Find one face. Then walk on, plant, breathe, begin.", "model": "[Three long exhales. Walk on. Plant.] Good *evening*. || I'm going to *start* | by thanking the people who *aren't* on stage tonight. || The parents. | The *drivers*. | The ones who *listened* | to every practice, | in every *bedroom*.", "mistake": "He walks on with his heart pounding, starts speaking before he reaches the mic, and fires the welcome at twice his normal speed while a single sheet of paper rattles in his hand.", "remember": ["Exhale longer than you inhale.", "Call it readiness, not fear.", "Hold card, never a single sheet.", "Sip water; tongue to the roof of the mouth.", "Start slower than feels right."]}, "drills": [{"id": "body-6-d1", "type": "breath", "title": "The long exhale", "how": "Breathe in through the nose, pause briefly, then out slowly through pursed lips. Where the item gives a line, say it aloud on a fresh breath after the last cycle.", "items": ["Four cycles sitting, one hand on the belly: feel it move outward on the in-breath.", "Four cycles standing in your stance, shoulders still.", "Three cycles, then say: 'Good evening, and welcome.'", "Two physiological sighs, two sniffs in and one long exhale, then say: 'Let's begin.'", "Twenty seconds of brisk stepping on the spot, then three cycles, then say: 'Item one.'", "Three cycles, then your first line under your breath, then aloud."], "pattern": [4, 1, 7, 0], "checks": ["Was each exhale longer than the inhale?", "Did your shoulders stay down on the in-breath?", "Did the line after the breaths start slower than usual?", "After the stepping, had your breathing settled by the third cycle?"]}, {"id": "body-6-d2", "type": "mirror", "title": "Steady the hands", "how": "Frame your hands as well as your face. Do each instruction, then say the line while holding, and watch for tremor.", "items": ["Hold a single sheet of paper out in front. Say: 'Thank you all for coming.' Then swap it for a card and repeat.", "Press the palms together hard for five seconds, release, let the hands hang. Say: 'Good evening.'", "Squeeze both fists for five seconds, release, hands to home. Say: 'Before we start.'", "Rest your fingertips lightly on a table edge. Say: 'I'll open the meeting.'", "Hold a mic stand-in close, elbow tucked in to the body. Say: 'This next tune is a ballad.'", "Hands in home, still. Say: 'Thank you. || Good night.'"], "seconds": 15, "checks": ["Did the card show less tremor than the sheet?", "Did the hands look steadier after the press or squeeze?", "Was the elbow tucked in when holding the mic?", "Did your hands stay still while you spoke?"]}, {"id": "body-6-d3", "type": "mirror", "title": "The minute before", "how": "Run the routine one step per item, in order, with water and a card within reach. Every step ends with something said aloud.", "items": ["Stance, feet planted, knees soft. Say aloud: 'I'm excited.'", "Three long exhales through pursed lips, shoulders still, then count aloud slowly from one to five.", "Press the palms, release, hands to home. Say your first line under your breath.", "Sip water, tongue to the roof of the mouth. Say the first line aloud, quietly.", "Eyes on the lens, resting face. Say the first line at full voice, slower than feels right.", "Walk out of frame and back in, plant, breathe, and begin: 'Good evening.'"], "seconds": 12, "checks": ["Did you do every step in order?", "Did your shoulders stay down through the exhales?", "Was the first line slower than your instinct?", "Did you plant before speaking?"]}, {"id": "body-6-d4", "type": "pace", "title": "Start slow, heart up", "how": "Do twenty seconds of brisk stepping on the spot to raise your heart rate, then read each opening within the target range. Your sense of pace will run fast; trust the meter.", "items": ["Good *evening*. || Welcome to the *Spring* Concert. || I look after the *music* department, | and tonight is the best night of my *year*.", "Good evening, everyone. | I'll open the *meeting*. || We have a *quorum*, | and *one* apology.", "Thank you for coming tonight. | This is the Year Seven *parent* evening, | and it'll take about an *hour*.", "Thanks, everyone. || This next one is a *ballad*, | so you can put your drinks *down*.", "Good morning. || I'm here to talk about the *tour*, | and I'll be *brief*.", "It's my privilege | to present the *final* award of the evening."], "targets": {"wpm": [120, 150], "fillers": 0, "pauses": 2}, "checks": ["Were you inside the target range from the first sentence?", "Did you hold each || for a full beat?", "Had your breathing settled by the third opening?", "Was the first sentence the slowest one?"]}], "apply": {"source": "story", "task": "Read the story, then raise your heart rate with thirty seconds of brisk stepping. Run the minute-before routine and retell the story standing, starting slower than feels right with your hands still.", "seconds": 90, "checks": ["Did you run the full routine before the first word?", "Was your opening slower than your instinct?", "Did your hands stay steady and visible?", "Did you keep the story's events in order?"]}}]}, {"strand": "Tone", "intro": "Tone is what a room hears before it hears your words: whether you are sure, whether you are on its side, whether you can be rattled. This strand trains the vocal and verbal mechanics of authority, warmth, steadiness and lightness, first separately and then together, so the chair, the parent meeting and the mic between tunes all get the settled voice you already have behind the bass.", "units": [{"id": "tone-1", "title": "The sound of certainty", "goal": "He can deliver a decision so it sounds settled: unhurried, falling at the end, short, and without asking permission.", "teach": {"body": "Certainty in a voice is mostly a matter of endings. In English a statement normally finishes on a falling pitch, a terminal fall, and listeners hear that fall as closure: the thought is finished and the speaker stands behind it. A rise at the end of a statement, what linguists call a high rising terminal and Australians know as the upward inflection, turns the statement into a question. Among friends it means \"are you with me?\" In a council meeting it reads as \"is this all right?\", and the room starts deciding for you.\n\nFour things carry authority, and they work together. Pace: a speaker who is sure does not hurry, because nothing is chasing them. Slowing the key sentence, not the whole talk, is enough. Pitch drop: let the last stressed word sit lower than the first, and let the voice land rather than trail. Fewer words: certainty is economical. \"The rehearsal moves to Thursday\" carries more weight than three sentences on whether a move might be worth considering. No permission-seeking: cut the tags and softeners that hand the decision to the listener: \"if that's okay\", \"does that make sense?\", \"I just think\", \"sort of\", \"maybe\".\n\nWhat goes wrong is rarely the content. Under pressure you speed up, add qualifiers to head off objection, and lift the end of each line to check it has landed. Each of those is a small request for reassurance, and an audience hears the request before the point. A filled silence does the same damage: an \"um\" after a statement tells the room the statement was provisional.\n\nHow to do it. Decide the sentence before you start it, so you know where it ends. Put the stress on the word that matters, usually late in the line. Bring the pitch down across the last two or three syllables and stop. Then hold the silence for a beat: the pause after a falling statement is where the room accepts it. You already know this from the podium. A downbeat is not a question; the band comes in because the gesture is committed. Speech works the same way.\n\nLower pitch is not the goal in itself. Pushing the whole voice down sounds forced and tires the throat. Use your own range and simply finish at the bottom of it.", "model": "Thanks, everyone. | Two items tonight. || The first is the *budget*. | We are *over* on instrument hire, | and I have a *fix*. || The second is the festival *date*. | It *stays* where it is. || We'll start with the *budget*.", "mistake": "So, um, I guess the first thing, if that's okay, is maybe the budget? We're sort of a bit over, I think, and I might have a possible fix, if people are happy to hear it?", "remember": ["End every statement lower than it began.", "Slow the key sentence, not the whole talk.", "Cut every tag that asks permission.", "Say it once, then stop.", "Hold a beat after the fall."]}, "drills": [{"id": "tone-1-d1", "type": "read", "title": "Falling endings", "how": "Read each line aloud, stressing the starred word and bringing the pitch down through the last few syllables. Stop completely at each full stop.", "items": ["Concert dress is *black*. | Shoes included. ||", "Sectionals start at *eight*, | not ten past. ||", "The minutes are *accepted*. || We move to item *three*. ||", "This next tune is a *blues*. | Count of *four*. ||", "The trip is *confirmed*. | Forms are due *Friday*. ||", "We're taking it from bar *forty*, | brass *only*. ||"], "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did every sentence end lower than it began?", "Did the stress land on the starred word, not the first word?", "Did any line rise at the end?", "Did you stop cleanly, with no trailing \"so\" or \"um\"?"]}, {"id": "tone-1-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Strip the permission", "how": "Say the weak line as you might under pressure, then the strong line with a falling ending. Listen for which one sounds like a decision.", "items": ["I was just wondering if maybe we could possibly start a bit earlier? => We start at *seven*. ||", "I sort of feel like the brass might be a little loud, if that makes sense? => Brass, | *half* the volume. ||", "So I think, unless anyone objects, we might move on? => We'll move to item *four*. ||", "Is it okay if I just quickly say a couple of things about the concert? => Two things about the *concert*. ||", "I'm not sure, but I think the deadline might be Friday-ish? => The deadline is *Friday*. ||", "Sorry, does everyone maybe want to settle down a bit? => *Eyes* up here. | Thank you. ||"], "checks": ["Was the strong line shorter than the weak one?", "Did the strong line end on a fall?", "Did you keep the strong line free of \"just\", \"sort of\" and \"maybe\"?", "Could you hear the difference on playback, not only feel it?"]}, {"id": "tone-1-d3", "type": "hold", "title": "Land and hold", "how": "Say each line with a falling ending, then hold silence for the full count without moving, nodding or adding a word.", "items": ["The decision is *made*.", "We're not changing the *program*.", "That's the end of *rehearsal*.", "The festival goes *ahead*.", "Bar *twelve*. | From the pickup.", "The answer is *no*."], "hold": 3, "checks": ["Did you hold the silence for the full count?", "Did you resist adding a softener in the silence?", "Did your face stay still rather than apologetic?", "Did the last word sit at the bottom of your range?"]}, {"id": "tone-1-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Decisions, stated once", "how": "Make the decision in your first sentence, give one reason, and stop. No tags, no rising endings.", "items": ["Tell the band the concert dress code is changing.", "Tell the committee the festival will not add a second stage this year.", "Tell the department that reports are due a week earlier.", "Tell a jazz audience there will be no second set tonight.", "Tell the Year 10s that phones are going in the box during rehearsal.", "Tell the council the uniform levy stays where it is."], "seconds": 30, "prep": 10, "structure": "ONE", "targets": {"wpm": [120, 150], "fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Was the decision in the first sentence?", "Did you give one reason, not several?", "Did every sentence end on a fall?", "Did you stop when the point was made?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Read the passage aloud once as written. Then speak it again, cutting every softener and permission tag and ending every sentence on a fall.", "seconds": 60, "checks": ["Was the second version shorter than the first?", "Did any sentence rise at the end?", "Did you pause after the most important line?"]}}, {"id": "tone-2", "title": "Warm without soft", "goal": "He can sound kind and on the listener's side while keeping the falling endings and pace that make him credible.", "teach": {"body": "Social psychologists have found that people judge one another on two broad dimensions, warmth and competence, and they judge warmth first: is this person on my side? Authority without warmth is heard as cold; warmth without authority is heard as nice but not in charge. The aim is both, and they live in different parts of the sentence.\n\nWarmth lives at the start and in the middle. Three physical things carry it. The smile: spreading the lips slightly shortens the vocal tract and brightens the vowels, and listeners can hear a smile in a voice even on the phone. It needs only a small lift at the corners, the face you make when a student finally gets a passage. The onset: start words gently rather than with a hard glottal attack, so the first syllable arrives like a bow on a string rather than a slap. The melody: let the pitch move more through the middle of the sentence. A flat line sounds bored or guarded; a voice that rises and falls within the phrase sounds engaged.\n\nWarmth is also verbal. Use the person's name. Acknowledge what they have done or felt, briefly and specifically: \"you came straight from work\" is warmer than \"thank you so much for everything\". Talk about them before you talk about the issue.\n\nAuthority lives at the end. Keep the terminal fall. Keep the pace unhurried. That is the whole difference between warm and soft. Soft is what happens when kindness leaks into the endings: the sympathetic rise, the trailing off, the apology for raising anything at all.\n\nWhat goes wrong. The commonest mistake is trying to sound kind by adding words: \"so sorry\", \"just\", \"only a little thing\", \"no stress at all\". Each one shrinks the message and makes the listener wonder what you are not saying. The second is sing-song, a bright up-and-down that sounds like talking to a child; parents notice it instantly. The third is overcorrecting into gravity, so a good-news conversation sounds like a diagnosis.\n\nHow to do it. Before you speak, look at the person and let your face soften. Say their name. Make one specific acknowledgement. Then state your point at the same pace and with the same falling ending you would use in a meeting you chair. Warm in, firm out.", "model": "Mrs Nguyen, | thank you for *coming in* | straight from work. || I've *loved* having Lucas in the band this year. | He *listens*, | and that's rare at thirteen. || I want to talk about his *practice*, | because I think he's *ready* for more.", "mistake": "Oh hi, so sorry to drag you in, it's nothing bad, honestly! I just wanted to have a little chat about practice, if that's okay?", "remember": ["Warm in the middle, certain at the end.", "Let your face soften before the first word.", "Name the person, then make the point.", "Acknowledge once, specifically. Never apologise for raising it.", "Kindness never costs you a falling ending."]}, "drills": [{"id": "tone-2-d1", "type": "repeat", "title": "Same line, three temperatures", "how": "Say each line three times: first neutral, then with a slight smile and gentle onset, then with the smile kept but a firm falling ending. Only the third is the target.", "items": ["Thanks for *staying back*. || I need ten minutes.", "You played that *beautifully*. || Now let's make it *in time*.", "I know it's been a *long* term. || Reports are due *Monday*.", "Welcome, everyone. || It's *good* to see a full room.", "Ella, | that was *brave*. || Let's do it again, | *louder*.", "I can see you're *tired*. || We'll finish at *four*."], "checks": ["Could you hear the smile in the second and third versions?", "Did the third version still end on a fall?", "Did the first word start gently, without a hard attack?", "Did the middle of the sentence move in pitch?"]}, {"id": "tone-2-d2", "type": "mirror", "title": "Find the smile", "how": "Prop the phone at eye level and do each instruction, watching your face. Say the line given in the instruction aloud at the end.", "items": ["Let your jaw hang loose and your lips part. Breathe through the mouth.", "Lift the corners of the mouth a few millimetres, no teeth. Check the eyes have softened too.", "Hold that face and say \"Good evening, everyone\" on a falling ending.", "Drop the smile completely and say it again. Notice the change in the vowels.", "Bring the small smile back and say \"Thank you for coming in\" to the camera as if it were a parent.", "Keep the smile and say \"The answer is no\". Check your eyebrows did not lift into an apology."], "seconds": 12, "checks": ["Did the smile reach the eyes, not just the mouth?", "Could you hear the brighter vowels when you smiled?", "Did your eyebrows stay level on the firm line?", "Did your jaw stay loose throughout?"]}, {"id": "tone-2-d3", "type": "swap", "title": "Warm, not soft", "how": "Say the soft version, then the warm version. The warm one should be shorter, more specific and end on a fall.", "items": ["So sorry to bother you, it's just a tiny thing about practice? => Lucas is *ready* for more practice, | and I want to help him *get* there. ||", "No stress at all, but maybe the forms could come back sometime? => Thanks for sorting the *trip*. || The forms are due *Thursday*. ||", "Oh, it was really, really good, everyone was amazing, honestly! => The *ending* was the best you've played it. || The *start* needs work. ||", "I hope it's okay that I asked you here, it's nothing bad? => Thanks for *coming*. || It's good news, | mostly. ||", "Sorry, I know everyone's tired, sorry, can we maybe go once more? => You're *tired*, | and it's *nearly* there. || Once more. ||", "Thanks so much, guys, you're all just the best, seriously. => Thank you for *giving up* a Saturday. || It showed. ||"], "checks": ["Did the warm version include one specific acknowledgement?", "Was the warm version free of apology words?", "Did the warm version end on a fall?", "Did you smile on the warm version without going sing-song?"]}, {"id": "tone-2-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Kind and clear", "how": "Acknowledge the person specifically, bridge to what matters, and give your answer warmly with a firm ending.", "items": ["A Year 7 tells you she's thinking of quitting clarinet.", "A parent apologises for missing every concert this year.", "A new staff member asks if they did something wrong in the meeting.", "A Year 12 thanks you after their final performance.", "A volunteer at the festival says nobody told them where to be.", "A student cries after a bad audition."], "seconds": 45, "prep": 15, "structure": "ABA", "targets": {"wpm": [115, 145], "fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did you open with something specific to them?", "Did you avoid saying sorry unless you had done something?", "Did your endings fall even on the gentle lines?", "Did you keep your pace unhurried?"]}], "apply": {"source": "story", "task": "Read the story, then retell it aloud. Let the moments where someone is kind sound warm, and tell the facts plainly with falling endings.", "seconds": 60, "checks": ["Could you hear a change in warmth between the human moments and the facts?", "Did you keep the endings down throughout?", "Did you name people rather than say \"this person\"?"]}}, {"id": "tone-3", "title": "Firm and warm together", "goal": "He can say no or deliver bad news in one or two sentences that are kind to the person and immovable on the decision.", "teach": {"body": "The skill is to aim warmth and firmness at different targets. Warmth goes to the person: their effort, their disappointment, their reasons. Firmness goes to the decision. Mix them up and you get either a cold no (firm at the person) or a wobbling no (warm about the decision, which tells them it can be reopened).\n\nThe negotiator William Ury calls the well-made refusal a \"positive no\", shaped yes, no, yes: affirm what matters to them, say no plainly, then offer what you can. \"I want Jack to grow as a soloist. He hasn't got this solo. I'll work with him on the next one.\" Each part is short. The no is a full sentence on its own, with a falling ending and nothing hedging it.\n\nBad news follows the same logic. Medical training in delivering bad news has a long-established shape: a brief warning that something hard is coming, then the news itself, early and in plain words, then silence to let it land. The common failure is the wind-up: three sentences of context, gratitude and \"it was so close\" before the news, during which the listener's anxiety climbs and the news, when it comes, sounds like an admission.\n\nThree verbal mechanics help. Use \"and\" where you would say \"but\". \"You've worked hard, but you're not in Band 1\" cancels the first clause; \"you've worked hard, and you're not in Band 1 this year\" lets both stand. Give one reason, not five; a list of reasons sounds like a defence and gives the listener five things to argue with. Leave out \"unfortunately\" and \"I'm afraid\"; they add drama, not kindness.\n\nVocally, let the warmth sit in the first clause, with the softer onset and some pitch movement, and let the pitch settle and fall through the decision. The decision clause should be the steadiest, slowest part of what you say. No rise, no laugh, no trailing off.\n\nThen stop. The silence after bad news belongs to the listener. Filling it with more explanation or comfort is usually for your own relief, and it reopens the decision. Wait, let them respond, and answer what they actually say. If they push, repeat the decision in the same words and the same tone. Repetition at an even pitch is the clearest signal that the matter is closed.", "model": "I know how much Jack *wanted* the solo, | and I'm glad he *went* for it. || He *hasn't* got it this time. || He has the *bridge*, | and it's *his*. || I'll work on solos with him | next *term*.", "mistake": "So, look, there were so many great auditions, honestly it was so close, and unfortunately, I'm so sorry, but Jack didn't quite get it this time?", "remember": ["Warm to the person, firm on the decision.", "Deliver the news in the first two sentences.", "Say \"and\", not \"but\".", "One reason, not five.", "Then stop, and let them respond."]}, "drills": [{"id": "tone-3-d1", "type": "read", "title": "Yes, no, yes", "how": "Read each refusal aloud. Let the first clause be warm, make the no the slowest and lowest part, and offer the alternative with a fall.", "items": ["I can see why you'd *want* that. || The answer is *no*. || What I *can* do is Thursday.", "Your son clearly *loves* drums. || He's not in the *jazz* band this year. || He's first in line for *next* year.", "Thanks for *thinking* of us. || We can't play the fundraiser. || I can send you *two* students who can.", "I value the *work* you've put in. || We're not funding it this round. || I'd like to see it *again* in March.", "You've *earned* a break. || Rehearsal still runs to *five*. || We'll finish *early* next week.", "It's a *great* idea. || Not this term. || Bring it to the *planning* day."], "checks": ["Was the no the slowest, lowest sentence?", "Did the warm clause sound genuinely warm, not polite?", "Did the alternative end on a fall, not a hopeful rise?", "Did you avoid \"unfortunately\" and \"I'm afraid\"?"]}, {"id": "tone-3-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Get to the news", "how": "Say the wound-up version, then the steady version. The steady version gives the news by the second sentence.", "items": ["Thanks so much for coming, it's been a big year, there were so many applications, and, well, unfortunately the grant didn't come through. => I've got *hard* news. || The grant *didn't* come through. ||", "So, everyone played really well, and it was so close, but I'm afraid we didn't place. => We *didn't* place. || And that was our best performance this *year*. ||", "I've been thinking a lot, and talking to people, and it's tricky, but Max might move bands? => Max is moving to Band *2* next term. || Here's *why*. ||", "I really hate to do this, and it's not your fault at all, but we need to cancel. => We're *cancelling* Friday's gig. || The venue's *closed*. ||", "You've done heaps for us, and we're so grateful, but we have to find someone else for the role. => Thank you for three *years*. || We're giving the role to someone *new*. ||", "It's not ideal, and I know it's a pain, but the concert might be moving? => The concert moves to the *ninth*. ||"], "checks": ["Was the news in the first or second sentence?", "Did the steady version use fewer words than the wind-up?", "Did you leave a silence after the news?", "Did your voice stay level, not sorry-sounding?"]}, {"id": "tone-3-d3", "type": "repeat", "title": "Turn the dial", "how": "Say each line three times: first too soft (rising, apologetic), then too hard (clipped, cold), then both firm and warm. Hear the third as the target.", "items": ["I know you *tried*. || You're not in the ensemble this year.", "That's *generous* of you. || We won't be taking the donation.", "I *hear* you. || The decision stands.", "You've put a lot *in*. || The piece is coming *out* of the program.", "It *matters* to you, | I can tell. || We're not reopening the vote.", "Thanks for the *honesty*. || I still need the part learned by *Monday*."], "checks": ["Could you hear three distinct versions on playback?", "Did the third version keep warmth in the first clause?", "Did the third version's decision sound immovable?", "Did the third version avoid both the rise and the clip?"]}, {"id": "tone-3-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "The no said kindly", "how": "Acknowledge the person, bridge in one clause, and give the no or the news plainly. Then stop.", "items": ["A teacher asks to take six of your players out of rehearsal for a sports carnival.", "Tell the council the instrument grant was refused.", "Tell a parent their child is moving from Band 1 to Band 2.", "A festival artist asks for a fee you cannot pay.", "A Year 11 asks to skip the concert for a party.", "Tell your department that the camp is cancelled this year."], "seconds": 45, "prep": 15, "structure": "ABA", "targets": {"wpm": [110, 140], "fillers": 1, "pauses": 2}, "checks": ["Did the no or the news arrive within two sentences?", "Did you use \"and\" rather than \"but\"?", "Did you give one reason only?", "Did you stop and leave silence at the end?"]}], "apply": {"source": "scenario", "task": "Answer the scenario aloud as if the person were in front of you. Kind to them, unmoved on the decision.", "seconds": 60, "structure": "ABA", "checks": ["Was the decision stated plainly, without a hedge?", "Was there one specific acknowledgement of the person?", "Did you resist over-explaining after the decision?", "Did the decision sentence end lowest?"]}}, {"id": "tone-4", "title": "Matching and leading rooms", "goal": "He can meet a room at its current state for a few seconds and then move it, by voice, to the state the meeting needs.", "teach": {"body": "Moods are contagious. Researchers in emotional contagion have shown that people unconsciously pick up the pace, posture, facial expression and vocal energy of those around them, and that this happens in both directions. The practical consequence is that you can move a room's state with your own, but only once the room feels you have noticed where it is. Start too far from them and they do not follow; they decide you have not understood.\n\nSo the method has two phases. Match: for the first few seconds, meet the room's intensity and seriousness, not its panic or its hostility. Lead: then change your pace, pitch and volume by small steps, and the room comes with you. You do this on the bandstand all the time: you play to the room for the first tune before you take it somewhere.\n\nThe anxious parent. They arrive fast and high. Bright cheerfulness feels dismissive; matching their panic confirms it. Meet their seriousness with a voice a little slower and lower than theirs, and name the feeling: \"you're worried\". Research on affect labelling has found that putting a feeling into words tends to reduce its intensity. Then slow further, drop the pitch, give one fact, and they settle with you.\n\nThe bored class. Fourth period Friday, low energy. You do not match boredom by being boring; you match by acknowledging it in one dry line, then lead with contrast: a sudden quiet, a faster instruction, an immediate task. Energy follows change, not volume.\n\nThe tense committee. Match the seriousness: quiet, measured, no jokes yet. Name what is at stake in one sentence. Then lead by giving structure, \"three things, in this order\", because tension is often uncertainty about what happens next.\n\nWhat goes wrong. Mismatching: arriving with your own state and expecting the room to adopt it. Over-matching: staying in their state too long, so you end up raising your voice with an angry parent or slumping with a flat staff meeting. The match is brief, ten seconds or so; the lead is gradual.\n\nHow to do it. Before you speak, read three things: pace, volume, and where people are looking. Open just inside their range. After the first few sentences, change one thing, usually slower and lower, and watch for bodies following. If they have not moved, hold the new level rather than pushing harder.", "model": "You're *worried* he's falling behind, | and I can hear it's been *building*. || Here's what I'm *seeing*. | He's behind on *two* pieces, | not *all* of them. || That's *fixable*. || Let's talk about *how*.", "mistake": "Oh, don't worry at all, he's totally fine, honestly! Lots of kids are behind, it's no big deal, it'll sort itself out!", "remember": ["Match for ten seconds, not ten minutes.", "Match intensity, never panic or hostility.", "Name the feeling before the facts.", "Lead by small steps: slower, lower, clearer.", "If they haven't moved, hold, don't push."]}, "drills": [{"id": "tone-4-d1", "type": "repeat", "title": "Three rooms, one line", "how": "Say each line three times: to an anxious parent, to a bored class, to a tense committee. Change pace, volume and pitch to match each room's starting state.", "items": ["Here's what *happens* next.", "I've got *good* news and *less* good news.", "We have *twenty* minutes. || Let's use them.", "Something's *changed*.", "Let's *start* again.", "Thank you for *waiting*."], "checks": ["Were the three versions clearly different on playback?", "Was the anxious-parent version slower and lower than your normal voice?", "Did the bored-class version have contrast, not just volume?", "Did the committee version sound measured rather than tense?"]}, {"id": "tone-4-d2", "type": "pace", "title": "Settle the room", "how": "Read each passage as if a tense room were in front of you. Start slightly brisk and slow into the target range by the second sentence.", "items": ["Right. | I know there's a lot of *feeling* in the room. || We have *three* items. | We'll take them *in order*, | and everyone will be *heard*.", "I can see people are *worried* about the budget. || So let's look at the *actual* numbers. | They're less *alarming* than the rumour.", "It's been a *hard* week for this ensemble. || Before we play a note, | here's the *plan* for today.", "I know the email came as a *shock*. || I'm going to tell you what was *decided*, | and *why*, | and then I'll take questions.", "Let's *breathe* for a second. || Nothing's being decided *tonight*. || Tonight we just *listen*.", "The tension's *understandable*. || Here's what we *know*, | and here's what we *don't*, | yet."], "targets": {"wpm": [105, 135], "fillers": 0, "pauses": 3}, "checks": ["Did your pace fall into the target range by the second sentence?", "Did your pitch settle lower as you went?", "Did you pause where the markup asked?", "Did the end of each passage sound calmer than the start?"]}, {"id": "tone-4-d3", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Meet them, then move them", "how": "Acknowledge the room's state in one sentence, bridge, then lead it somewhere specific with your voice.", "items": ["A parent rings, upset that their child came home crying after rehearsal.", "The Year 9 class has just come in from a hot lunch and nobody's listening.", "The council is split and the last speaker raised their voice.", "Staff meeting, week ten, everyone is exhausted.", "The audience is restless after a long interval.", "The jazz crowd is chatting over the band's quiet ballad."], "seconds": 45, "prep": 15, "structure": "ABA", "checks": ["Did your first sentence show you had noticed the room?", "Did you change pace or pitch after the first sentence?", "Did you lead them to a specific next step?", "Did you avoid staying in their state too long?"]}, {"id": "tone-4-d4", "type": "recall", "title": "Turn the room", "how": "Study the anchors, then speak the talk in order without looking. Match the starting state in the first anchor and lead through the rest.", "items": ["Name the mood / Say why it makes sense / One fact that changes the picture / The next step / What you need from them", "It's a hard decision / The worry is about fairness / Here's how places were decided / Appeals close Friday / Ask me after", "It's late and hot / Five minutes, not fifty / One run of the coda / Then we're done / Thank you for staying", "This room disagrees / Both views want the same outcome / Here is the choice in one sentence / We vote at the end / Speak now if you haven't", "You're anxious about the trip / I've run it six times / Here's the supervision plan / Here's my number / You'll hear from me each night"], "seconds": 60, "prep": 20, "targets": {"wpm": [110, 145], "fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did you deliver the anchors in order?", "Did the first anchor match the room's state?", "Was your voice slower and lower by the last anchor than the first?", "Did you finish without a trailing \"so, yeah\"?"]}], "apply": {"source": "direction", "task": "Say the direction three times: to a room that is anxious, one that is bored, and one that is tense. Match each for a sentence, then deliver the direction so the room moves.", "seconds": 45, "checks": ["Did each version open differently?", "Did all three end on the same firm direction?", "Did you change one thing at a time when leading?"]}}, {"id": "tone-5", "title": "Steady under fire", "goal": "He can take a hostile question, an interruption or a public contradiction and answer it at an even pitch, pace and volume.", "teach": {"body": "Being challenged in public triggers the body's threat response before the thinking brain has caught up. The breath moves high into the chest, the larynx tightens, pitch rises, pace quickens and the hands start to move. The room reads every one of those signals, and they read as \"rattled\". Steadiness is mostly about managing the first two seconds, before any words.\n\nStart with the breath. Heart rate naturally slows slightly on each exhale, so a long, quiet out-breath through the nose is the one physiological lever you control in the moment. Exhale, then answer. The pause this creates also reads well: an answer that arrives after a beat sounds considered; an answer that arrives on top of the question sounds defensive.\n\nAnswer the question, not the tone. A hostile question usually contains a fair question wrapped in loaded words. Find the fair question and answer that. Restating it neutrally helps: \"the question is where the money went\" rather than \"you're asking why I lost the money\". Never repeat the loaded word, even to deny it. \"It wasn't wasted\" puts \"wasted\" in everyone's ear.\n\nThe ABA structure (acknowledge, bridge, answer) gives you a shape when your mind is racing. Acknowledge in a few words: \"fair question\". Bridge: \"here's what happened\". Answer, with a fact.\n\nInterruptions. If you are mid-point and it matters, finish the sentence at the same volume, then turn: \"let me finish this, then you're next\". Do not get louder; get slower. A voice that drops while someone talks over it sounds in command. If the interruption is useful, yield cleanly and come back: \"go on\", then \"to finish my point\".\n\nBeing contradicted in public. Correct once, calmly, with a source: \"that's not my understanding. The minutes from March say otherwise.\" Then move on. Arguing the point twice turns a correction into a contest. As chair you also have procedure: \"we'll take that in general business\" is steadier than any argument.\n\nWhat goes wrong. Matching hostility. Over-defending, with three justifications where one fact would do. The nervous laugh. Rising pitch. Speeding up to get it over with. And going cold, which reads as wounded.\n\nThe target is the voice you would use to give the time of day: level, unhurried, finished.", "model": "*Fair* question, | and I'm glad it's been *asked*. || The money was *moved*, | to cover the bus hire in *June*, | and that's in the *minutes*. || I'll walk you through the figures | *after* the meeting. || Next item.", "mistake": "No, no, hang on, that's actually completely unfair, because if you'd read the minutes, which everyone got, by the way, you'd know it was the bus hire!", "remember": ["Exhale before you answer.", "Answer the question, not the tone.", "Never repeat the loaded word.", "Get slower and lower, never louder.", "Correct once, cite the source, move on."]}, "drills": [{"id": "tone-5-d1", "type": "breath", "title": "Exhale first", "how": "Follow each instruction for several cycles. The exhale is longer than the inhale; keep the shoulders still.", "items": ["Breathe in through the nose low into the belly, then out through the nose, slow and quiet.", "Put a hand on your lower ribs and feel them widen on the in-breath.", "On the out-breath, let your jaw and tongue go loose.", "Imagine a hostile question has just been asked. Take one full cycle before speaking.", "On the last out-breath, say \"Fair question\" at the bottom of the breath, low and level."], "pattern": [4, 1, 6, 1], "checks": ["Did your shoulders stay down on the in-breath?", "Was the exhale audibly longer than the inhale?", "Did your voice on the last cycle sound lower than usual?"]}, {"id": "tone-5-d2", "type": "hold", "title": "The considered beat", "how": "Imagine each line is your answer to a hostile question. Exhale, say the line level and falling, then hold the silence without adding to it.", "items": ["That's not my *understanding*.", "Let me *finish* the point.", "The minutes say *otherwise*.", "Go on. | I'm *listening*.", "We'll take that in general *business*.", "I've answered that. || Next *question*."], "hold": 3, "checks": ["Did you hold the full silence without filling it?", "Did each line stay at speaking volume, not louder?", "Did your pitch stay low and level?", "Did your face stay neutral, not tight or smiling?"]}, {"id": "tone-5-d3", "type": "swap", "title": "Rattled to level", "how": "Say the rattled answer as it might come out under pressure, then the level one slowly. Notice which loaded words disappear.", "items": ["No, it was not wasted, the money was not wasted at all! => The money went to bus *hire*. || It's in the *minutes*. ||", "Sorry, can I just, sorry, can I finish, please? => Let me *finish*, | then you're *next*. ||", "That's just wrong, that's completely wrong, I never said that. => That's not what I *said*. || I said *Thursday*. ||", "Well, I mean, I'm not trying to favour anyone, honestly, I'm really not. => Places were decided by *audition*. || The criteria are *published*. ||", "Ha, okay, look, I don't know why everyone's attacking me here. => I can see people are *frustrated*. || Here's what *happened*. ||", "You can't just say that in a meeting, that's not fair! => I'll *respond* to that once. || Then we'll move *on*. ||"], "checks": ["Did the level version avoid repeating the loaded word?", "Was the level version slower and lower than the rattled one?", "Did the level version contain a fact or a source?", "Did you resist laughing nervously?"]}, {"id": "tone-5-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Under fire", "how": "Read the hostile question, exhale, then answer it with acknowledge, bridge, answer. Level pitch, even pace, no loaded words repeated.", "items": ["\"Why does your band always get the best rehearsal times?\"", "\"Isn't it true the festival lost money last year under your watch?\"", "\"My daughter says you play favourites. What do you say to that?\"", "\"With respect, you're a musician, not a finance person. Why are you treasurer?\"", "\"That's not what you told us last term.\" (said across you mid-sentence)", "\"Why should the school keep funding a program this expensive?\""], "seconds": 40, "prep": 5, "structure": "ABA", "targets": {"wpm": [110, 140], "fillers": 1, "pauses": 2}, "checks": ["Did you take a breath before your first word?", "Did you avoid repeating the hostile phrasing?", "Did your volume stay at conversation level?", "Did you answer with at least one fact?", "Did you stop once you'd answered?"]}], "apply": {"source": "scenario", "task": "Treat the scenario as a hostile moment in front of a room. Exhale, then answer aloud, level throughout.", "seconds": 60, "structure": "ABA", "checks": ["Did you pause before answering?", "Did your pitch stay level across the whole answer?", "Did you answer the fair question inside the hostile one?", "Did you finish without justifying twice?"]}}, {"id": "tone-6", "title": "Humour and lightness", "goal": "He can use a dry line or a light touch to ease a room, judge when not to, and deliver it so it lands without being pushed.", "teach": {"body": "Lightness tells a room you are at ease, and a room that laughs together relaxes and listens. It is also the easiest tone to get wrong, because a joke that misfires costs more than the silence it replaced.\n\nResearch on humour styles draws a useful line. Affiliative humour, which brings people in on a shared joke, and self-enhancing humour, the ability to see the funny side of your own situation, go with good relationships. Aggressive humour, at someone else's expense, and self-defeating humour, putting yourself down to be liked, tend to damage them. That gives the rule: aim at the situation, the weather, the instrument, the timetable, or yourself in small doses. Never aim at someone in the room, least of all someone with less power: a student, a volunteer, a parent.\n\nSelf-deprecation works when it comes from security. One line from the person running the meeting reads as confidence. A habit of it reads as self-defeating, and it quietly undermines the authority you built in the first unit.\n\nThe dry line is your natural register and suits Australian rooms. It runs on understatement, which rhetoric calls meiosis: saying less than the situation deserves. \"The forecast for the outdoor concert is mixed\" while the rain hammers the roof. Its delivery is deadpan: no smile, no lift in pitch, the same falling ending as a fact. The room has to find it, and finding it is the pleasure.\n\nTiming is mechanical. Put the funny word last, which is end-weight, so the laugh comes when the sentence finishes, not in the middle of it. Leave a small pause before the payoff word. Then stop. Do not explain, do not laugh first, do not add a second joke to rescue the first. If it misses, carry on at the same pace as if it were a statement, because to the room it now is one. A callback, returning to something said earlier in the evening, gets a bigger response than a new joke, because the room is in on it.\n\nWhen it helps: openings, transitions, the moment after tension has broken, between tunes at a jazz gig. When it hurts: before bad news, which makes the news sound dismissed; in grief; in reply to a hostile question, where it turns into sarcasm; and as an inside joke that leaves half the room out.", "model": "Thank you very much. || That was a *ballad*, | which in jazz is the one moment | the *bass* player gets heard. || We'll *fix* that now. || This next one's by *Monk*, | and it's *fast*.", "mistake": "Ha, so that was a ballad, which, ha, means the bass player gets heard for once, haha, because, you know, bass players, right? Anyway.", "remember": ["Put the funny word last, then stop.", "Deliver it flat; let them find it.", "Aim at yourself or the situation, never someone present.", "One self-deprecating line, not a habit.", "If it misses, keep going at the same pace."]}, "drills": [{"id": "tone-6-d1", "type": "read", "title": "The dry line", "how": "Read each line deadpan: no smile, no pitch lift, a pause before the payoff, and a falling ending as if stating a fact.", "items": ["The Year 7 band has rehearsed this since *February*. || So have their parents, | through a *bedroom* door.", "The forecast for tonight's outdoor concert | is *mixed*.", "We've had a *small* change to the program. || Most of it.", "The treasurer's report is short, | which is the *best* kind.", "That tune is in seven-four. || I counted *most* of it.", "Welcome to the AGM, | the highlight of everyone's *social* calendar."], "checks": ["Did you keep a straight face through the payoff?", "Did the payoff word come last and fall?", "Did you pause just before the payoff?", "Did you resist laughing or smiling after?"]}, {"id": "tone-6-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Joke surgery", "how": "Say the overworked joke, then the trimmed one. The trimmed version is shorter, ends on the funny word and is not explained.", "items": ["Ha, so, this concert, it's been, like, a bit of a journey, let's say, ha, with all the, you know, rehearsals and stuff! => It's been a *long* road to tonight. || Mostly *uphill*. ||", "I'm terrible with technology, seriously, I'm the worst, you guys, I'm like a dinosaur! => If the slides work, | that's the *students*. ||", "So the budget, ha, I'll just say it's not great, it's a bit of a disaster really, ha! => The budget is *interesting*. || Treasurers choose that word *carefully*. ||", "Okay, this next one's in a weird time signature, so if we fall apart, haha, sorry in advance! => The next one's in *five*. || So are we, | *hopefully*. ||", "Thanks for coming, I know it's freezing, I'm literally dying out here, ha. => Thank you for braving the *weather*. || We've hidden the heaters for the *band*. ||", "Sorry, I talk too much, I'm a total rambler, my partner always says, anyway. => I'll be *brief*. || Relatively. ||"], "checks": ["Was the trimmed version half the length or less?", "Did it end on the funny word?", "Did you deliver it without a laugh in your voice?", "Was the target the situation or yourself, never someone present?"]}, {"id": "tone-6-d3", "type": "hold", "title": "Wait for it", "how": "Deliver each line dry, then hold for the laugh you imagine. Do not fill the silence, nod, or step on it.", "items": ["Thank you. || We've been *the band*.", "We're running *slightly* late. || By jazz standards, | we're *early*.", "Item four is the *uniform* policy. || I can feel the *excitement*.", "Our next piece has a *lot* of notes. || We'll play *some* of them.", "The raffle prize is a *keyboard*. || Please take it *home*.", "That was our *final* number. || Unless you *clap*."], "hold": 2, "checks": ["Did you hold the silence for the full count?", "Did you stay still and neutral while holding?", "Did the line itself end on a fall, not a lift?", "Did you move on cleanly after the hold?"]}, {"id": "tone-6-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Open light, then land", "how": "Open with one light line aimed at the situation or yourself, then make your single real point plainly. Stop after the point.", "items": ["Open the Year 9 concert on a night of heavy rain.", "Start a council meeting that's running half an hour late.", "Introduce the next tune after a string breaks on stage.", "Begin the department meeting in the last week of term.", "Open the parent evening when the projector won't work.", "Close the awards night after a very long program."], "seconds": 40, "prep": 20, "structure": "ONE", "targets": {"wpm": [115, 150], "fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Was there exactly one light line?", "Was it aimed at the situation or yourself?", "Did you shift to a plain, falling delivery for the real point?", "Did you stop without explaining or adding a second joke?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Read the passage aloud as written. Then find one place where a single dry line would help the room, add it, and deliver the whole passage again with that line deadpan.", "seconds": 75, "checks": ["Did the added line target the situation or yourself?", "Did you deliver it flat, with the payoff word last?", "Did the rest of the passage keep its falling endings?", "Was it one line, not two?"]}}]}];
